Members of the National Assembly for Wales fear confusion with the Muppets
In the case of some members of the National Assembly, the proposal to change their title to “Member of the Welsh Parliament” (Member of the Welsh Parliament) triggers dismay.
The reason for this is plans to change the name of the National Assembly to “Welsh Parliament” (Welsh Parliament).
National Assembly members of all political directions fear to become the target of ridicule.
A National Assembly member belonging to the Labour Party said his group was worried because the name "rhymes with 'Twp' and 'Pwp'."
For non-Welsh readers: In Welsh, “twp” stands for “doof” and “pwp” for “Aa”.
A national assembly member of the Plaid Cymru party stated that the entire group was “not happy” and had proposed alternatives.
A member of the Welsh Conservative Party said his group was open to the name change, but pointed to the great similarity between "MWP" and "Muppet."
In this context, the Welsh letter (w) is pronounced similar to the letter u in English spoken in Yorkshire.
The Assembly Commission, which is currently drafting a law for the name changes, said: “The final decision on designations for the National Assembly members is, of course, with the members themselves.”
The Government of Wales Act of 2017 authorized the Welsh National Assembly to change its name.
In June, the panel published the results of a public hearing on the proposals that gave rise to broad support for the name “Welsh Parliament” for the National Assembly.
With regard to the titles of the members of the National Assembly, the board preferred “Welsh Parliament Members” (Welsh Members of Parliament) or “WMPs”, while a public hearing the option “MWP”.
National Assembly members are apparently proposing alternative options, however the struggle for a consensus for Chairman Elin Jones, who is due to submit a bill within weeks, could pose a problem.
The Reform Act will contain further changes regarding the functioning of the National Assembly, including rules to disqualify National Assembly members and to design the committee system.
The members of the National Assembly may vote definitively on their name during their debate on the law.
Macedonians vote in a referendum on a change in the country's name
Voters will decide on Sunday whether their country's name should be changed to "Republic of North Macedonia."
The referendum was scheduled to settle a decades-long dispute with neighboring Greece, which has a province with the similar name Macedonia.
Athens has insisteded for a long time that the name of its northern neighbor means that it lays entitles to the Greek territory, and has repeatedly spoken out against its accession to the EU and NATO.
Macedonian President Gjorge Ivanov, an opponent of the referendum on the name change, said he would not recognize the vote.
Supporters of the referendum, however, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that the name change was the price payable for joining the EU and NATO.
The bells of St. Martin's are silent in the face of the difficulties of the churches in Harlem
"The old people I was talking to said there had been a bar and a church on every corner in the past," Adams said.
“There aren’t both anymore.”
He said the disappearance of the bars was understandable.
“People’s societal lives look different today,” he said.
“Bars are no longer the living rooms of the neighborhood, where people find themselves regularly.”
At the churches, he fears that the money from the sale of assets won't last as long as the church leaders assume, and that they "sooner or later will be back at the beginning."
Churches, he added, could be replaced by apartment buildings with condominiums where people live who don't support the remaining refuges.
“The overwhelming majority of people buying condominiums in these buildings will be whites,” he said, “and thus accelerating the complete closure of these churches, as it is unlikely that most of the people who move into these condominiums will become members of these churches.”
Both churches were erected by white congregations before Harlem became a black metropolis: Metropolitan Community in 1870, St. Martin's a decade later.
The original white Methodist community moved out in the 1930s.
A black congregation that held its services nearby became the owner of the building.
St Martin's was taken over by a black community under Reverend John Howard Johnson leading a boycott by retailers on 125th Street, a major shopping street in Harlem that refused to hire or support blacks.
During a fire in 1939, the building was severely damaged, but when Father Johnson's parishioners planned to rebuild, they commissioned the chimes.
Reverend David Johnson, son of father Johnson and his successor at St. Martin's, proudly referred to the chimes as "the bells of the poor."
The experient who rang the Glockenspiel in July used other words: “a cultural treasure” and “an irreplacable historical instrument”.
The expert, Tiffany Ng of the University of Michigan, also noted that it was the world's first chimes played by a black musician, Dionisio A. Lind, who had moved 18 years ago to the larger chimes at the Riverside Church.
Merriweather said he hasn't been replaced by St Martin's.
What has transpired at St Martin's in recent months is a complicated history with architects and contractors, some of whom have been consulted by the lay leaders of the church and in part by the episcopal diocese.
In July, the municipality’s board of directors consisting of lay leaders – expressed written concerns to the diocese that the diocese would try to pass on the costs to the municipal board, although it had not been involved in commissioning the architects and contractors sent by the diocese.
Some parishioners complained about a lack of transparency on the part of the diocese.
Shark injures 13-year-old in California while diving after lobstern
In California on Saturday, the first day of the lobster season, according to official statements, a shark attacked a 13-year-old boy while diving to Lobstern.
The attack took place shortly before 7:00 a.m. near Beacon's Beacon's Beach in Encinitas.
As Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV in San Diego, he had been diving with friends for about half an hour on Saturday morning when he heard the boy's cries for help and paddled with several others to help pull him out of the water.
Hammel said he first thought the boy was happy to have caught a lobstery, but then he realized that the boy screamed “I was bitten!
“I have been bitten!”
When Hammel arrived at the boy's, he found that his entire collarbone had been torn open.
“I screamed that everyone should come out of the water, ‘There’s a shark in the water!’,” Hammel added.
The boy was flowned to Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego and is in critical condition.
The nature of the shark responsible for the attack was unknown.
Lifamous swimmist Captain Larry Giles told the media that a few weeks earlier a shark had been seen in the area.
Giles added that the victim had suffered traumatic injuries in the upper-boss area.
Officers suspended beach access from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami's in Encinitas for 48 hours for investigative purposes and for safety reasons.
According to Giles, there are over 135 species of sharks in the area, but for the most part they are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury's plans foray into UK cosmetics market
Sainsbury's is attacking boots, superdrugs and debenhams with cosmetics departments that look at department stores staffed with specialized assistants.
As part of a large-scale foray into Britain's £2.8 billion cosmetics market, which continues to grow while fashion and household goods sales decline, the larger cosmetics divisions will be tested in eleven stores nationwide. If they prove successful, they will be expanded to more stores next year.
The investment in cosmetics coincides with the supermarkets' search for ways to otherwise use retail space previously used for televisions, microwave appliances and household goods.
According to Sainsbury's, for the first time, the scope of its cosmetics offering doubles to up to 3,000 products, including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr PawPaw.
Existing series from L'Oreal, Maybelline and Burt's Bees also get more space and brand areas similar to those in stores like Boots.
In addition, the supermarket is carrying out a relaunch of its “Boutique” make-up series, making most of the products vegan-friendly – an aspect increasingly demanded by younger customers.
In addition, perfume retailer Fragrance Shop will test sales outlets in two Sainsbury's stores. The first outlet opened last week in Croydon in south London, and a second will open later this year in Selly Oak, Birmingham.
Online shopping and the transition to the daily purchase of small amounts of food at local consumer goods stores means that supermarkets need to do more to get customers to visit them.
According to Mike Coupe's CEO of Sainsbury's, outlets will increasingly look like department stores as the supermarket chain is trying to battle with more services and non-food items against discounters Aldi and Lidl.
Sainsbury's has set up Argo's sales outlets in hundreds of stores two years ago and a number of Habitat outlets since the Argos and Habitat chains two years ago, which has boosted food revenues as well as the profitability of takeovers.
The supermarket's previous attempt to reorganize its cosmetics and pharmacy departments failed.
Sainsbury's tested a joint venture with Boots in the early 2000s, which, however, ended after an altercation over the distribution of revenues from the drugstore's stores in the supermarkets.
The new strategy follows Sainsbury's sale of its 281-store pharmaceutical business to Celesio, the owner of Lloyds Pharmacy chain, for £125 million three years ago.
The company stated that Lloyds will play a role in the plan by adding an expanded range of luxury skincare brands such as La Roche-Posay and Vichy in four stores.
Paul Mills-Hicks, commercial director at Sainsbury's, said: "We have redesigned the appearance of our cosmetics departments to create a more attractive environment for our customers.
In addition, we have invested in specially trained colleagues who are available as consultants.
Our range of brands is tailored to all needs. Due to the enticing environment and convenient locations, we are now an attractive destination for cosmetic purchases that challenges the previous type of shopping.”
Peter Jones 'furious' after Holly Willoughby withdraws from £11 million worth of business
“Dragons’ Den” star Peter Jones is “angry” after TV presenter Holly Willoughby retired from a £11 million business with his lifestyle brand company to focus on her new contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
Willoughby doesn't have time for joint homewear and accessory brand Truly.
The business of the two was compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand.
“This Morning” presenter, 37, announced her retreat on Instagram.
Holly Willoughby enraged “Dragons’ Den” star Peter Jones through her extremely short-term withdrawal from her lucrative lifestyle brand company to focus on her own new follow-up contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
Jones is said to have been "angry" when television's "Golden Girl" at a tense meeting Tuesday at the headquarters of its Marlow, Buckinghamshire's headquarters, admitted not being able to devote enough time todage to joint homewears and accessories brand Truly due to their new businesses - worth up to £1.5 million.
The company was compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand and would probably have doubled Willoughby's estimated fortune of £11 million.
While Willoughby, 37, announced her withdrawal from Truly on Instagram, Jones left Britain on his way to one of his vacation homes.
One source said: "Truly was by far the highest priority for Holly.
It should secure their future for them in the long term over the coming decades.
Their decision to withdrew came as a complete surprise to everyone involved.
No one could believe what happened on Tuesday, so close to the market launch.
At the headquarters in Marlow, a warehouse full of goods is waiting to be selled.”
Experts believe the withdrawal of "This Morning" presenter, who is one of Britain's most promisiving stars, could cost the company millions due to high investment in products ranging from pillows and candles to clothing and homewear and possible further delays in launch.
What's more, it could mean the end of a long friendship.
Willoughby, mother of three children, and her husband Dan Baldwin have been close friends with Jones and his wife Tara Capp for ten years.
Willoughby founded Truly in 2016 with Capp, and Jones, 52, joined as chairman in March.
The couples spend the holiday together, and Jones holds a 40 per cent stake in Baldwin's TV production company.
Willoughby is to become a brand ambassador for M&S and replace Ant McPartlin as presenter of the ITV show "I'm A Celebrity".
A Jones-affiliated source said last night: "We don't comment on his business matters."
