The Cromwell Society, however, described Crick's suggestion as "folly" and "attempt to rewrite history."
John Goldsmith, chairman of the Cromwell Society, said: "It was inevitable in the current debate over the removal of statues that Oliver Cromwell's stylle was aimed.
Cromwell's storm was neither ordered nor carried out by Cromwell.
Possibly the false Cromwell would be sacrificed for the actions of his ancestor Thomas in the previous century
Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft's magnificent depiction of Cromwell is proof of the 19th-century prevailing opinion and part of the historiography of a character who, in the opinion of many, is still worth celebrating.
Goldsmith told the Sunday Telegraph: "Cromwell is regarded by many, perhaps at the end of the 19th century more than today, as someone who defended parliament against pressure from the outside, in its case, of course through the monarchy.
Whether this is a completely accurate depiction is the subject of continued historical debates.
What is certain is that in the middle of the 17th century conflict shaped the subsequent development of our nation, and Cromwell is a single recognizable figure depicting one side of this divide.
