Killer pig bites Chinese farmers dead
According to local media reports, a farmer at a market in southwest China was attacked and killed by a pig.
The man, to whom only the surname was called “Yuan”, was found dead with a severed artery covered in blood near a pigstary at the market in Liupanshui in Guizhou province, reported the South China Morning Post on Sunday.
A pig farmer prepares on May 30, 2005 in Xining in Qinghai province, China, to inject pig vaccines.
He had reportedly arrived on Wednesday with his cousin from the neighboring province of Yunnan to sell 15 pigs at the market.
The next morning his cousin found him dead and saw that a door to a neighboring pigsty was open.
He said there was a large male pork with blood on his snout in the stable.
According to the report, a forensic investigation confirmed that the 250-kilo pig had bitten the farmer to death.
“My cousin’s legs were bloody and mutilated,” the cousin, to whom the surname was called “Wu,” was quoted as saying by the Guiyang Evening News.
Footage from a security camera showed Yuan entering the market at 4:40 a.m. Thursday to feed his pigs.
His body was found about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man did not belong to Yuan or his cousin.
A market manager told Evening News the pig had been imprisoned to prevent attacks on more people while police gathered evidence at the scene.
Yuan's family and the market authorities are reportedly negotiating compensation for his death.
While it is rare for pigs to attack humans, such cases are known.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband at her Massachusetts farm. The man suffered serious injuries in the process.
Ten years ago, a 290-kilo pig trapped a Welsh farmer between himself and a tractor until the farmer's wife could scare away the animal.
After a farmer was eaten by his pigs in Oregon in 2012, a farmer from Manitoba told CBC News that pigs weren't usually aggressive, but that the taste of blood could act as a "trigger."
“They’re just playful.
They like tweaking and are very curious ... they'ren't looking to hurt anyone.
You just have to treat them with the right level of respect,” he said.
Remains of Hurricane Rosa bring widespread heavy rain to the Southwestern U.S.
Hurricane Rosa weakens on its way over the colder water on the northern coast of Mexico.
Rosa, however, will bring overflooding rains in northern Mexico and the Southwestern U.S. in the coming days.
On Sunday at 5:00 a.m., Rosa had wind speeds of 136 km/h, equivalent to a Category 1 hurricane, located 620 kilometers southwest of Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
Rosa is expected to move north on Sunday.
In the meantime, a trough begins to form over the Pacific that moves east to the west coast of the U.S. When Rosa reaches the Baja California peninsula as a tropical storm on Monday, high tropical moisture will be transported north to the southwestern U.S.
Rosa will trigger up to 25 centimeters of rainfall in parts of Mexico on Monday.
Then the tropical humidity generates widespread heavy rainfall in the southwest in the coming days through interactions with the approaching trough.
Locally, 2.5 to 10 centimeters of rainfall can cause dangerous flood-like floodings, water-driving rubble and possibly landslides in the desert.
High tropical humidity sometimes leads to precipitation rates of nearly 5 to 7.5 centimeters per hour, especially in parts of southern Nevada and Arizona.
In the Southwest, especially in a large part of Arizona, up to 5 to 10 centimeters of rainfall is expected.
Due to the spread of tropical rainfall, there is the possibility of flood-like flooding with rapidly deteriorating conditions.
In view of the looming tropical rainfall, it is strongly discourageed from walking into the desert.
Severe rainfall can cause canyons torrencing streams, and thunderstorms bring locally wind gusts as well as wind that carries dust.
The approaching trough brings locally heavy rain to parts of the coast of Southern California.
Total precipitation quantities of more than one centimeter are possible, which can lead to a small extent to debris floating in the water and to smooth roads.
This would be the first rainfall of the rainy season in this region.
The first isolated tropical rain showers reach Arizona on Sunday night and Monday morning. On Monday night and Tuesday, it rains widely.
On Tuesday, heavy rain spreads to the Four-Corners region and lasts Wednesday.
In October, severe temperature fluctuations are possible throughout the U.S. as the Arctic cools, while the tropics remain relatively warm.
This sometimes leads to drastic temperature differences over short distances.
On Sunday, there's a good example of drastic temperature differences in the middle of the U.S.
Between Kansas City, Missouri, and Omaha, Nebraska, as well as between St. Louis and Des Moines, Iowa, there is a temperature difference of nearly 12 oC.
Over the next few days, the ongoing summer heat is trying to develop and expand again.
For a large part of the middle and the east of the U.S., a warm October beginning with widespread temperatures around 27 oC is expected from the Southern Plains to parts of the northeast.
New York City could reach 27 oC on Tuesday, which is about 6 degrees above average.
Our long-term climate forecast indicates a high probability of above-average temperatures in the first half of October in the east of the USA.
Over 20 million viewers have seen Brett Kavanaugh's hearing
Over 20 million viewers have seen the captivating statements of Supreme Court-nominated Brett Kavanaugh and the woman Christine Blasey Ford on six television networks on Thursday.
In the meantime, the political stalemate persisted. The television stations on Friday interrupted their regular program because of a last-minute twist: an agreement negotiated by Jeff Flake, an Arizona senator, according to according to which the FBI is to conduct a week-long investigation into the allegations.
Ford told the Senate Judiciary Committee to be 100% certain that Kavanaugh had been drunk at a high school party, afflicted her and tried to remove her clothes.
Kavanaugh said in a passionate statement that he was 100% sure this had not happened.
It's likely that more than the 20.4 million viewers reported by Nielsen have watched the hearing.
The company determined the average viewership at CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
For other networks like PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network that aired the hearing, the numbers weren't immediately available.
In addition, Nielsen usually has trouble measuring viewer numbers in offices.
To put this in perspective: This roughly corresponds to the number of spectators at a play-off football game or at the Oscar ceremony.
Fox News Channel, whose opinion-oriented presenters strongly support Kavanaugh's nomination, recorded the highest numbers of all networks with an average of 5.69 million viewers of the full-day hearing.
ABC came second with 3.26 million viewers.
CBS had 3.1 million, NBC 2.94 million, MSNBC 2.89 million and CNN 2.52 million viewers.
Interest remained high after the hearing.
Flake was the central figure in Friday's drama.
After the office of the moderate Republican released a statement that he would vote for Kavanaugh, he was filmed on Friday morning by cameras from CNN and CBS as protesters loudly afflicted him with words in the elevator on the way to a Judiciary hearing.
He stood for minutes with his eyes lowered while he was scolded. The scene was broadcast live on CNN.
"I'm standing right in front of you," said one woman.
“Do you think he’s telling the country the truth?”
“They have power while so many women are powerless,” one woman said.
Flake said his office had issued a statement and supplemented before the elevator concluded that he would have more to say at the hearing before the committee.
All cable and broadcast networks broadcast live hours later, when the Judiciary Committee was scheduled to vote on forwarding Kavanaugh's nomination to the entire Senate.
However, Flake said he would do so only on the condition that the FBI, as requested by the Democratic minority, is investigating the accusations against the candidate next week.
Flake was partially persuaded by talking to a friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
After an interview with Coons and subsequently various senators, Flake made his decision.
Flake's decision was of great importance as it was obvious that Republicans wouldn't have enough votes without the investigative for Kavanaugh's confirmation.
President Trump has prompted an FBI investigation into the accusations made against Kavanaugh.
British Prime Minister May accuses critics of using Brexit for political purposes
Prime Minister Theresa May, in an interview with the Sunday Times newspaper, accused critics of her plans for leaving the European Union of using Britain's future for political purposes and undermining the nation's interests.
British Prime Minister Theresa May arrives at the Conservative Party Congress in Birmingham on September 29, 2018.
In another interview published next to the interview with her on the front page of the newspaper, her former Foreign Secretary Boris Johnson tightened his attack on her so-called "Chequers plan" for Brexit by describing a proposing a proposal that provides for the mutual collection of tariffs by Britain and the EU as "completely absurd."
Fatal shots on Wayde Sims: Police arrest suspect Dyteon Simpson in connection with LSU player's death
Police have arrested a suspect in connection with the fatal shooting of Wayde Sims, a 20-year-old basketball player at Louisiana State University (LSU).
Dyteon Simpson, 20, has been arrested and imprisoned for manslaughter by Baton Rouge, according to Baton Rouge police.
The agency released video footage of the altercation between Sims and Simpson. According to the police, Sims lost his glasses during the fight.
According to the CBS partner station WAFB, the police secured the glasses at the scene and said DNA had been found on Simpson's glasses.
According to Simpson's interrogation, police said he confessed to the deadly shot.
The Advocate reports that the deposit had been set at $350,000.
The East Baton Rouge Parish Coroner's Office released a preliminary report on Friday, according to which the cause of death was a gunshot wound to the neck.
Police are thanking the Louisiana State Police Fugitive Task Force, the Criminal Investigation Laboratory of Federal Police, the Southern University Police and citizens in the arrea for their assistance in the investigation leading to the arrest.
LSU sporting director Joe Alleva thanked the regional enforcement agency for “its care and its pursuit of justice.”
Sims was 20 years old.
The 1.98 m winger grew up in Baton Rouge, where his father Wayne had also playeed for LSU basketball.
Last season he scored an average of 5.6 points and 2.6 rebounds per game.
On Friday morning, LSU's basketball coach Will Wade said the team was "deeply shaken" by Wayde's death and was "in shock."
"You're always afraid of something like this," Wade said.
Volcancola spewed ashes on Mexico City.
Ash ejected from the Popocatepetl volcano has reached the southern neighborhoods of the Mexican capital.
The National Center for Disaster Prevention warned Mexicans on Saturday to stay away from the volcano after activity in the crater increased and 183 ejections of gas and ash were registered within 24 hours.
The authority oversaw numerous cases of rumbles and tremors.
Pictures on social media showed thin layers of ash on the windshields of cars in neighborhoods of Mexico City, such as Xochimilco.
Geophysicists noted increased activity on the volcano located 72 kilometers southeast of the city of the city since a 7.1 central Mexico earthquake in September 2017.
Known as “Don Goyo”, the volcano has been active since 1994.
Clashes between police and Catalan separatists ahead of anniversary of independence vote
In Barcelona, six people were arrested on Saturday after clashes between riot police and pro-independence protesters. Thousands took part in other demonstrations to commemorate the first anniversary of the polarising independence referendum in Catalonia.
A group of masked pro-separatists, held back by the riot police, threw eggs at them and also hurled color powder, creating dark clouds of dust in the streets normally populated with tourists.
In the further course of the day there were also handcuffs with the police, who used their batons to contain the fighting.
For several hours, pro-independence groups chanted “No Forgetting, No Forgiving,” while Unionists confronted them shouting “Long Live Spain.”
As the local press reported, fourteen people were treated because of minor injuries suffered in the protests.
One year after the October 1 referendum, which was seen by Madrid as illegal but celebrated by separatist Catalans, the situation in the independent region remains very tense.
Voters had overwhelmingly opted for independence even though turnout was low as secession opponents mostly boycotted the vote.
According to Catalan authorities, nearly 1,000 people were injured in the last year when police tried to prevent voting in violent clashes outside polling stations.
Pro-independence groups had camped outdoors overnight on Friday to prevent a demonstration for national police.
The demonstration took place but was forced to take a different route.
Narcis Termes, 68, an electrician who took part in the separatists' protest with his wife, said he sees the prospects that Catalonia would gain independence no longer with hope.
“Last year we experienced one of our best moments.
I saw my parents crying for joy because they could vote, but now we're stucking," he said.
Despite an important, albeit scarce, victory in last December's regional elections, the Catalan pro-independence parties have struggled to maintain the momentum this year. Many of their best-known leaders are either in self-imposed exile or in detention, where they are awaiting their proceedings because of their role in organising the referendum and the subsequent declaration of independence.
Joan Puig, a 42-year-old mechanic who recorded the protest in favor of police on his phone, said the conflict had been stoked by politicians on both sides.
"The situation is becominging increasingly tense," he said.
On Saturday, Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders who have been in pre-trial detention since the end of last year, announced his candidacy for the election to the European Parliament next year.
“Competing as a candidate for the European elections is the best way to denounce the dismantling of democratic values and repression by the Spanish government,” he said.
Londonderry: Men arrested after house was rammed by car
Three men, aged 33, 34 and 39, were arrested after a Londonderry home had been rammed by a car on several occasions.
The incident took place in Ballynagard Crescent at 7:30 p.m. on Thursday after British summer time.
Detective Inspector Bob Blemmings said the gates and the building itself were damaged.
In addition, it may have been shot at the car with a crossbow.
Menga hit means 1:0 win over Rangers for Livingston
Dolly Menga's first goal for Livingston secured victory.
The ascended Livingston FC surprised the Rangers and leaded to their only second defeat for Steven Gerrard in 18 games as manager of the Ibrox club.
Dolly Menga's hitter proved to be crucial for Gary Holt's squad to equal Hibernian in the second half.
Gerrard's squad will remain in the Premiership this season without an away win and face the Hearts next Sunday, who are eight points behind.
Previously, the Rangers will host Rapid Vienna in the Europa League on Thursday.
Livingston, meanwhile, can increase his winning streak in the division to six games, that is, head coach Holt has yet to record a defeat since his takeover of Kenny Miler last month.
Livingston misses chances against outspoken playing guests
Holt's team should have been in the lead long before the hit, as his directness put the Rangers with a variety of problems.
Scott Robinson broke through but slowed down in front of goal. Then Alan Lithgow could only shoot wide after slipping to hit Craig Halkett's headshot past goal.
The hosts were content to let the Rangers play in front of them as they knew they were able to hamper guests in standard situations.
In this way, the decisive goal came about.
The Rangers allowed a free-kick, and Livingston prepared a chance where Declan Gallagher and Robinson put Menga in shooting position together. The latter scored the goal with a ball contact from the middle of the penalty area.
At this stage, the Rangers had dominated possession but could not overcome the hosts' defence, and goalkeeper Liam Kelly remained largely unmolested.
This pattern continued in the second half, although Alfredo Morelo's once forced Kelly to defend.
Scott Pittman was fended off by Rangers goalkeeper Allan McGregor with his feet, and Lithgow slapped the ball over from a different standard stay by Livingston.
Flanks kept getting into Livingston's penalty area and were regularly taken out of the danger area, while two required penalty kicks - one by Halkett against interchanged Glenn Middleton and one because of hand-play - were denied.
“Phenomenal” by Livingston
Alasdair Lamont of BBC Scotland at Tony Macaroni Arena
Phenomenal performance and a strong result for Livingston.
They were invariably outstanding and exceeded the expectations in this upward trend again.
Her style of play and her team has hardly changed since their return to the top teams, but Holt deserves great praise for the way in which he has shaken up the team since his arrival.
He's had so many heroes.
Captain Halkett was great and led an excellently organised defence, while Menga Kept Connor Goldson and Joe Worrall on their toes throughout the game.
Rangers, however, lacked inspiration.
As good as they were at times under Gerrard, they could not reach this standard.
Their last ball was inadequate – only once were they able to penetrate the hosts’ squad – and that’s something like a wake-up call for the Rangers, who are in the middle of the standings.
Receiving Erdogan with mixed reactions in Cologne
On Saturday (September 29), smiling faces and blue skies prevailed as the heads of government of Turkey and Germany met in Berlin for breakfast.
It was the last day of President Erdogan's contentious visit to Germany, which was intended to repair relations between NATO allies.
There had been disputes over issues such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey's EU accession.
Erdogan traveled to Cologne to open a large new mosque.
The city is home to Turkey's largest population.
The police cited security reasons for stopping a 25,000-strong crowd from gathering outside the mosque. However, many supporters gathered nearby to see their president.
Hundreds of anti-Erdogan protesters – many of them Kurds – also made themselves heard. They condemned Erdogan’s policies as well as the German government’s decision to welcome him to the country.
The opposing protests reflect the polarizing effect of the visitor, praised by some German Turks as a hero and reviled by others as an autocrat.
Deptford road accident: cyclist dies in collision with car
A cyclist has died in a collision with a car in London.
The clash occurred at about 10:15 a.m. British daylight saving time near the intersection of Bestwood Street and Evelyn Street, a bustling road in Deptford in the south-east of the city.
The driver of the car stopped, and paramedics took care of the man, but who died at the scene.
Months before the accident, another cyclist had died in an accident involving driver's escape on Childers Street, about 1.6 kilometres from Saturday's accident.
According to the Metropolitan Police, officials were working to identify the man and notify his nearest relatives.
Road closures and diversions for buses have been set up, and motorists have been advised to avoid the area.
Long-Lartin prison: Six officers injured in riots
According to the Prison Office, six prison officers were injured in riots at a maximum-security men's prison.
The riots erupted on Sunday at about 9:30 a.m. British summertime at HMP Long Lartin in Worcestershire and are stilling.
Specialized “tornado” officials were consulted to end the riots involving eight inmates and who are confined to one wing.
Officers were treated at the scene for minor injuries to their faces.
A prison service spokesperson said: "Specially trained prison staff have been deployed to remedy an ongoing incident at HMP Long Lartin.
Six employees have been treated for injuries.
We do not tolerate violence in our prisons and would like to make it clear that those responsible will be handed over to the police and will potentially spend longer time behind bars.”
HMP Long Lartin is home to more than 500 convicts, including some of the country's most dangerous offenders.
In June, it was reported that the prison manager had been treated in hospital following an attack by a convict.
In October last year, emergency officials were summoned to the prison to crack down on serious unrest in which personnel were attacked with poolar balls.
Hurricane Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City with flood-like flooding (drought areas may benefit)
It's rare for a tropical depression to reach Arizona, but that's what's likely to happen early next week, when the remnants of Hurricane Rosa move over the desert-influenced Southwest and entail the risk of flood-liking flooding.
The National Weather Service has already issued warnings of flood-like flooding on Monday and Tuesday from western Arizona to the south and east of Nevada, as well as to the southeast of California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to be approaching with rain on Monday nighting and moving directly across Phoenix on Tuesday.
The National Weather Service introduced in a tweet that “since 19550, only ten tropical cyclones as a tropical storm or tropical depression Phoenix have come close up to 320 kilometers!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane within 64 kilometres from the Arizona border.”
The latest models from the National Hurricane Center predict 5 to 10 centimeters of rainfall with occasionally up to 15 centimeters in Arizona's Mogollon Rim.
In other areas of desert-influenced south-west, including central Rockies and the Great Basin, 2.5 to 5 centimeters are probable and occasionally a total of up to 10 centimeters.
For those who do not have the risk of flood-liking flooding, the rainfall associated with pink can be a blessing due to the drought in the region.
Although flooding is of great concern, this rain is likely to be useful in part, as there is currently a drought in the southwest.
According to the U.S. Drought Monitor, at least extreme drought occurs in just over 40 percent of Arizona, the second-highest category," weather.com reported.
To begin with, the path of the hurricane means that Rosa will hit land on the Baja California peninsula in Mexico.
On Sunday morning, Rosa will still have hurricane strength with maximum winds of 136 kilometers per hour and are located 620 kilometers south of Punta Eugenia, Mexico. Subsequently, the hurricane will move north at 19 kilometers per hour.
In the Pacific, the storm will hit for colder waters, weakening as a result.
Therefore, Rosa is expected to hit land on Monday afternoon or evening in Mexico with the strength of a tropical storm.
Precipitation can be severe in parts of Mexico, resulting in a significant risk of flooding.
“From Baja California to the northwest of the Sonoran Desert, precipitation totals of 7.5 to 15 centimeters are expected, whereby up to 25 centimeters is possible,” weather.com reported.
Subsequently, as a tropical storm in a northerfront direction, will move over Mexico early Tuesday morning as a tropical depression as a tropical depression and then move upwards to southern Utah by late Tuesday night.
“The main danger in connection with pink or its remains is very heavy rainfall is expected in Baja California, the northwest of the Sonoran Desert and the desert-influenced Southwest of the United States,” the National Hurricane Center said in a statement.
As a result of this rainfall, life-threatening floods are expected in the desert and landslides in the water, as well as in mountainous areas.
Midsomer Norton attack: Four arrested for attempted murder
Following the discovery of a 16-year-old with stab wounds in Somerset, three teenage boys and a 20-year-old man were arrested on suspicion of attempted murder.
The teenager was found injured at around 4:00 a.m. British daylight saving time in the Excelsior-Terrace area of Midsomer Norton on Saturday.
He was taken to a hospital, and his condition is “stable.”
According to Avon and Somerset police, a 17-year-old, two 18-year-old and a 20-year-old man were arrested overnight in the Radstock area.
The officers asked all those who have records of what happened on a mobile phone to come forward.
Trump said Kavanaugh had suffered “under the meanness, anger” of Democrats.
"A vote for Judge Kavanaugh is a voice against the unscrupulous and outrageous tactics of Democrats," Trump said at a rally in Wheeling, West Virginia.
Trump said Kavanaugh had suffered “under the meanness, anger” of Democrats throughout the nomination process.
Kavanaugh testified before Congress on Thursday, dismissing the allegation made by Christine Blasey Ford that he sexually assaulted her decades ago when both were teenagers.
Ford also testified at the hearing on her allegation.
The president said on Saturday that the American people had seen the brilliance, quality and courage of Kavanaugh on that day.
“A voice for the affirmation of Judge Kavanaugh is a vote for the confirmation of one of the most experienced legal minds of our time, a jurist with an excellent record in service to the general public,” he told the crowd in West Virginia.
The president was indirectly referring to Kavanaugh's nomination when he talked about the importance of Republican voters' involvement in the midterm elections.
