Trump argued that Democrats saw their mission at “resistance and block.”
The first major procedural vote on Kavanaugh's nomination is expected to take place in the Senate by Friday at the latest, a senior GOP executive told CNN.
Hundreds killed in Earthquake in Indonesia
According to Saturday's authorities, at least 384 people were killed. Many of them were swept away when gigantic waves struck the beaches due to a strong earthquake and a tsunami on the Indonesian island of Sulawesi.
Hundreds of people had come together at a feast on the beach of the city of Palu on Friday, when waves up to six-meter-high struck the beacon at nightfall, tearing many people to death, destroying everything that stood in their way.
The tsunami was preceded by an earthquake of magnitude 7.5.
“When the tsunami threat arose yesterday, people were still on the beach about their activities and didn’t immediately run away, and they fell victim to him,” said Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, spokesman for the Indonesian civil protection agency BNPB at a conference in Jakarta.
“The tsunami didn’t come alone, it flooded cars, logs and houses with, it hit everything on land,” Nugroho said, adding that the tsunami had moved across the open sea at a speed of 800 km/h before hitting the coast.
Some people had climbed trees while fleeing from the tsunami and had survived, he said.
About 16,700 people were evacuated and housed in 24 centres in Palu.
Aerial photographs published by the civil protection agency showed numerous destroyed buildings and shops, bent and collapsed bridges as well as a mosque surrounded by water.
On Saturday, the coastal town was rocked by further aftershocks.
The Earthquake series was felt in an area of 2.4 million people.
Indonesia's BPPT (Agency for the Assessment and Application of Technology) said in a statement that the energy released in Friday's massive quake had matched 200 times the energy of the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima in World War II.
Due to the city's location at the end of a long, narrow bay, the tsunami could have got bigger, the authority said.
Nugroho described the damage as “comprehensive” and stated that thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels had collapsed.
According to his information, the bodies of some victims were founded under the debris of collapsed buildings. 540 people were injured and 29 are missing.
Nugroho said the number of casualties and the damage could have been higher along the coast 300 km north of Palu, in an area called Donggala, located closer to the quake's epicenter.
The communication with Donggala, according to Nugroho, was completely paralyzed, and there was no information.
According to a statement from the Red Cross, more than 300,000 people live there, and its associates and volunteers are on their way to the affected areas.
"This is already a tragedy, but it could get much worse," the statement said.
On Saturday, the authority was frequently criticized for failing to inform that a tsunami had hit Palu, even though the waves arrived at the time of the warning, according to official data.
Amateur footage posted on social media shows a man on the upper floor of a building desperately warning people in the street below him about the approaching tsunami.
Within minutes, a wall of water crashes onto the shore and floods buildings and cars away.
Reuters could not immediately verify the authenticity of the footage.
As a result of the quake and the tsunami, there was a massive power outage that interrupted communication around Palu and made it more difficult for the authorities to coordinate the rescue efforts.
According to authorities, the military has begun sending cargo planes with support from Jakarta and other cities, but evacues still desperately in need of food and other essential goods.
The city's airport will remain closed until October and has only been reopened for relief efforts.
A visit by President Joko Widodo to evacuation centers in Palu was scheduled for Sunday.
The number of tsunami casualties has risen to more than 800.
The situation is very bad.
World Vision employees from Donggala have safely reached the city of Palu, where employees are housed in tent shelters in the courtyard of their office, but, according to Doseba's words, passed scenes of devastation along the way.
"They said they had seen numerous destroyed houses," he said.
The situation is very bad.
Even as the relief groups began the tough task of starting disaster relief, some complained that expert foreign aid workers would be prevented from travelling to Palu.
According to Indonesian law, financial aid, supplies and personnel from abroad are only possible if the site of an accident has been declared a national disaster area.
That hasn't happened until now.
“At the moment, it’s still a disaster at provincial level,” said Aulia Arriani, spokeswoman for Indonesia’s Red Cross.
“As soon as the government says, ‘okay, this is a national disaster’, we can accept international support, but so far this status does not apply.”
As the second night descended over Palu since Friday's earthquake and tsunami, friends and family members of the still-missing of those who were hoped for one of the miracles that brighten up the sad stories of natural disasters.
On Saturday, a young boy was pulled out of a sewer.
On Sunday, rescue workers freed a woman pinched under debris next to her mother's corpse for two days.
Gendon Subandono, the coach of Indonesia's paragliding national team, had coached two of the missing paragliders for the Asian Games that ended earlier this month in Indonesia.
Others of the prisoners at the Roa-Roa-Hotel were, as Mandagi supplemented, its students.
“As a senior in paragliding, I’ve got my own emotional burden,” he said.
Gendon recounted how, in the hours after the news of the Roa-Roa hotel's collapsing among paragliders, he desperately sent WhatsApp messages to the competition participants from Palu taking part in the beach festival.
However, however, his messages only led to gray question marks instead of blue ticks.
"I think that means the news hasn't been delivered," he said.
Thieves steal $26,750 while replenishing an ATM in Newport on the Levee
According to a Newport police press release, thieves stole $26,750 from Brink''s employee in replenishing an ATM on Friday morning.
The vehicle's driver had emptied an ATM in the entertainment complex and wanted to add more money, Detective Dennis McCarthy wrote in the press release.
While was employed, “a man came running behind the Brink’s collaborator and stole a sack full of money destined for the vending machine.
According to the press release, witnesses saw several suspects flee from the crime scene. However, police did not specify how many people were involved in the incident.
Individuals with information on the identity of the suspects are asked to contact Newport police at 859-292-3680.
Kanye West: Rapper changes his name to Ye
Rapper Kanye West changes his name to Ye.
When announcing the change on Twitter on Saturday, he wrote: "The being officially known as Kanye West".
West, 41, has nicknamed Ye for some time and has used it as the title of his eighth album released in June.
The change precedes his appearance on Saturday Night Live, where the release of his new album “Yandhi” is expected.
He performs in the show as a replacement for the singer Ariana Grande, who canceled for “emotional reasons”, according to the producer.
The word is not only an abbreviation of its current professional name, but according to its own statement it has a religious meaning for West.
“I think ‘ye’ is the word most commonly used in the Bible, and in the Bible it means ‘you,’” West said in a conversation this year about his album with radio host Big Boy.
“So I’m you, I’m us, it’s us.
Kanye, which means ‘the only one’, became only ‘Ye’ – simply a reflection of our good, our bad, our confused, of everything.
The album rather reflects what we are.”
He's one of several famous rappers who changed their name.
Sean Combs was known as Puff Daddy, P. Diddy and Diddy, but announced this year that he prefers the names Love and Brother Love.
JAY-Z, whom West used to collaborate, has already written his name with and without hyphen and capital letters.
Mexico's AMLO vows not to use the military against the civilian population
President-elected, Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador, vowed to never use military force against the civilian population while the 50th anniversary of bloody reprisals against students is approaching.
Lopez Obrador pledged on Saturday at Tlatelolco Plaza "never to use the military to responder the Mexican people."
Soldiers had fired for a peaceful demonstration in the square on October 2, 1968, killing 300 people. At that time, left-leaning student movements were in the process of establishing themselves throughout Latin America.
Lopez Obrador vowed to support young Mexicans through monthly aid for students and the opening of more free public universities.
He said unemployment and lack of training opportunities were supplying young people with criminal gangs.
U.S. should redouble financial resources for artificial intelligence
In view of China's intensified activities in the field of artificial intelligence, the U.S. should redouble its spending on research in this field, says investor and AI practitioner Kai-Fu Lee, who worked at Google, Microsoft and Apple.
The comments follow AI announcements from various areas of the U.S. government, even if the U.S. generally lacks a formal AI strategy.
In the meantime, China unveiled its plan last year: the country intends to become the #1 in the field of AI innovation by 2030.
“Doubling the budget for AI research would be a good start, given that all other countries lag so much further behind the U.S.” We’re looking for the next AI breakthrough,” Lee said.
Doubling funding could double the chances that the next major AI success in the U.S. would be achieved, Lee said this week in an interview with CNBC.
Lee, whose book “AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order” was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in one of China’s most prominent AI companies, Face++.
In the 1980s, he worked at Carnegie Mellon University on an AI system that defeated America's highest-placed Othello player. Later, he was a senior executive at Microsoft Research and president of Google's branch in China.
Lee paid tribute to previous U.S. government technology competitions, such as the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency's (DARPA) Robotics Challenge, and asked about the timing of the next competition as he wanted to help identify the next visionaries.
According to Lee's words, researchers in the U.S. often have to work hard to receive government subsidies.
“It’s not China that’s taking away from top academic staff, it’s the companies,” Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have discontinued luminaries of universities designed to work on AI.
In Lee's opinion, a changed U.S. immigration policy could also be conducive to U.S. AI efforts.
“I think that in the field of AI doctorees, greencards should automatically be offered,” he said.
China's State Council unveiled its plan for the development of the next-generation Artificial Intelligence Development Plan in July 2017.
In Lee's words, the National Natural Science Foundation of China finances individuals at academic institutions similarly like the National Science Foundation and other governmental organizations lend money to U.S. researchers, but the quality of academic work in China is lower.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense established the Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, in which partners from industry and academia are said to be involved. In addition, the White House announced the formation of an artificial intelligence committee.
DARPA also announced $2 billion investment this month in the “AI Next” initiative.
The National Science Foundation (NSF) invests more than $100 million annually in AI research.
In the meantime, U.S. laws regarding the formation of the National Security Commission on Artificial Intelligence for months nothing has happened.
Macedonians vote in referendum on a change of country name
Macedonia's population voted Sunday in a referendum on whether to change the country's name to "Republic of North Macedonia." That measure would settle a decades-old dispute with Greece that had blocked Macedonia's requests for admission to the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, claims that the name of its northern neighbour means that the latter lays entitles to the Greek territory. Therefore, Greece has vetoed Macedonia's admission to NATO and the EU.
The two governments finalized an agreement in June on the basis of the proposed new name. However, nationalist opponents argued that the change would undermine the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority population of Macedonia.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he will not vote in the referendum. A boycott campaign puts in doubt that turnout reaches the 50 percent necessary for the validity of the referendum.
The question on the ballot was: “Are you in favour of membership of NATO and the EU by adopting the agreement with Greece?”
Supporters of the name change, which includes Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that for Macedonia, one of the countries resulting from the collapse of Yugoslavia, the inclusion in organizations such as the EU and NATO is worth the price.
“I have come today to vote for the future of the country, for young people in Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, since it means a safer life for all of us,” Olivera Georgijevska, 79, said in Skopje.
Although the result of the vote isn't legally binding, enough MPs have said they're sticking to the outcome so it's crucial.
The name change would require a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The state electoral commission said there had been no reports of irregularities until 1 p.m.
Voter turnout, however, was only 16 percent compared to 34 percent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, where 66 percent of registered voters cast their vote.
“I’m here because of my children to vote, our place is in Europe,” said Gjose Tanevski, 62, a voter in the capital Skopje.
Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko cast their votes in the referendum on changing the country's name that would open Macedonia's path to joining NATO and the European Union on September 30, 2018.
In front of the parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, prepared a small stage and put chairs in front of tents set up by people who wanted to boycott the referendum.
"We are for NATO and the EU, but we would like to join raised heads, not through the back entrance," Kavadarkov said.
“We are a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they don’t want to take us as Macedonia, we can turn to others such as China and Russia and become part of integration between Europe and Asia.”
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO's membership will result in much-needed investment for Macedonia, whose unemployment rate is more than 20 percent.
"I believe that a large majority will be in favour, as more than 80 percent of our citizens are in favour of the EU and NATO," Zaev said.
According to his words, a “yes” result would be a “confirmation of our future.”
According to a survey published on Monday by the Macedonian Institute for Policy Research, between 30 and 43 percent of voters would participate in the referendum. That figure is below the required turnout.
Another survey conducted by Macedonian broadcaster Telma TV found that 57 percent of respondents plan to vote on Sunday.
70 percent said they would vote “yes.”
For the referendum to be successful, turnout must be 50 percent plus one vote.
A failure of the referendum would be the first major blow to the pro-Western government's policies since taking power in May last year.
Sergio Aguero from Manchester City manoeuvres through the entire Brighton defence to score goal
Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling overcame Brighton's Defense in Manchester City's 2:0 victory on Saturday at the Etihad Stadium in Manchester.
Aguero made his hit look ridiculously easy in the 65th minute.
The Argentine attacker received a pass in midfield at the start of the sequence.
He raced between three Brighton defenders and subsequently ran into the open field.
Aguero then saw himself surrounded by four green jerseys.
He played around a defender and then overtook several other defenders on the edge of Brighton's penalty area.
Subsequently, he shot a pass to the left to Sterling.
The English attacker used his first ball contact in the penalty area to play the ball back to Aguero, who overcame Brighton goalkeeper Mathew Ryan with his right shoe and shot into the right side of the net.
“Aguero is struggling with problems in his feet,” City manager Pep Guardiola told reporters.
“We’d talked that he was supposed to play 55, 60 minutes.
That's what's happened.
We were lucky that he scored a goal at that moment.”
However, it was Sterling who gave the Sky Blues the initial advantage in the Premier League duel.
This goal fell in the 29th minute.
Aguero received the ball on that turn wide on Brighton's side.
He scored a good steep pass along the left flank to Leroy Sané.
Sané needed a few moves to steer Sterling to the long corner.
The Sky-Blues attacker targeted the ball into the net just before it slipped into the end.
City will play in the Champions League group game on Tuesday at 12:55 p.m. at the Rhein-Neckar-Arena in Sinsheim against Hoffenheim.
Scherzer wants to spoil the Rockie's game
Since the Nationals have retired from the playoff competition, there was little reason to force another stake.
However, the always-performing Scherzer hopes to stand on the hill against the Colorado Rockies on Sunday, but only if this is relevant for the Rockies, who have a game ahead of the Los Angeles Dodgers in the NL West for the playoffs.
The Rockies scored at least one wild-card with a 5:2 victory over the Nationals on Friday night but still want to secure their first division title.
“Although we don’t play for anything, we can at least throse as we know the atmosphere among the spectators here in Denver and the other team would probably play at the highest level I’d face this year.
Why don’t I want to participate in this competition?”
The nationals have not yet announced a starting player for Sunday, but are, according to reports, inclined to have Scherzer thrown in such a situation.
Scherzer, for whom this would be the 34th appearance as a starting player, practiced in the bullpen on Thursday and would throw on his normal rease on Sunday.
The Washington right-hander this season has an 18-7 statistic, an ERA of 2.53 and 300 strikeouts in 220 2/3 innings.
Trump holds rally in West Virginia
The president was indirectly referring to the situation related to Brett Kavanaugh chosen by him for the Supreme Court when he spoke about the importance of Republican voters' involvement in the midterm elections.
“Everything we’ve done is at stake in November.
Five weeks before one of the most important elections of our lives.
That’s one of the big ones – I’m not competing, but in reality I’m competing, that’s why I’m fighting for great candidates everywhere,” he said.
Trump continued, “You are seeing this terrible, horrible radical group of Democrats, you’re seeing it happening right now.
And they are determined to take power again by all means, you see the meanness, the abomination.
They don’t care who they hurt, who they have to run over to maintain power and control, that’s what they want, power and control, we won’t give them.”
Democrats, he said, saw their mission as “resistance and block.”
“And you’ve seen that in the last four days,” he said, describing Democrats as “angry and mean and abhorrent and dishonest.”
He named the highest-ranking Democrat in the Senate Judiciary Committee, Senator Dianne Feinstein, which provoked noisy booing calls to the audience.
“Do you remember your answer?
Did you let the document get to the outside?
Uh, uh, what.
No, uh no, I’m waiting for you – that was a really bad body language – the worst body language I’ve ever seen.”
Labour no longer allows all opinions to apply.
The party is intolerant of those who speak its mind.
When momentum activists in my local party voted to blame me, this came as little surprise.
After all, I'm the last of a number of Labour MEPs who are told they're not welcome - just because we're speaking our mind.
My Member of Parliament Joan Ryan was treated similarly as she had vigorously opposed anti-Semitism.
In my case I was criticised with the rebuke application for having a different opinion than Jeremy Corbyn.
On the importance of responsible economic policies, on national security, about Europe, ironically on similar issues that Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
The announcement of Friday's Nottingham East Labour meeting said "the meetings are intended to involve everyone and be productive."
Mostly the Friday evening GC meetings in my eight years as a local Labour MP were just that.
Today, unfortunately, most sessions aren’t shaped by this tone, and the promised “more friendly, more cautious” policy has long been forgotten, if it has ever begun at all.
It is becoming increasingly clear that different views are not tolerated in the Labour Party and that any opinion will be judged by whether it will be accepted by the party leadership.
This began shortly after Jeremy became party leader. Colleagues, with whom I previously believed to share similar political views, were now expecting me to make a U-turn and take positions that I would otherwise never have agreed with - whether in terms of national security or the European single market.
Whenever I speak publicly – although it doesn’t matter what I say – a scolding tirade follows on social media calling for my deselection, denouncing the political middle and saying I shouldn’t be a member of the Labour Party.
And I'm not alone with that experience.
In fact, I know that I am doing better than some of my colleagues, since the comments addressed to me are mostly political in nature.
I admire the professionalism and determination of colleagues who face a flood of sexist or racist insults on a daily basis and yet never add small.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this political age is that insults have become the normal.
Jeremy Corbyn demanded last week the Labour Party should promote a culture of tolerance.
In reality, we no longer allow all opinions to apply, and with every no-confidence motion and any amendment to the electoral rules, the party becomes more narrow-minded.
In the last two years I’ve been advised to move my head down and don’t express my opinion so clearly. Then I would “not have any problems.”
But I didn't go into politics for that.
Since as a pupil 32 years ago, prompted by the neglect of the Thatcher government, on the basis of which my classroom literally fell apart in the comprehensive school, have joined the Labour Party for better public services for those who need it most – as a local councillor as well as a cabinet minister.
I've never made a secret of my political views, even at the last election.
No one in Nottingham East could in any way be unclear about my political positions and areas in which I disagree with the current leadership.
I just want to say to the supporters of Friday's request: given that the country is rushinging towards a Brexit that will harm households, businesses and our public service, I don't understand the desire to waste time and energy on my loyalty to the leadership of the Labour Party.
In fact, however, I address this one message not to Nottingham Momentum, but to my constituents, regardless of whether they are Labour members or not: I am proud to serve you, and I promise that so many deselection threats or political sense of purpose will not stop me from acting in the best interest of all of you.
Chris Leslie is MP for Nottingham East
Ayr vs Melrose 38:17: Undefeated Ayr team advances to the top
Two late attempts may have distorted the end result somewhat, but there's no doubt that Ayr deserved victory in this hugely entertaining game of the day in the Tennent's Premiership.
They are now the only of the ten teams undefeated at the top of the standings.
In the end, the success of the home squad was due to their superior defence as well as their better exploitation of chances, and coach Peter Murchie had every reason to be satisfied.
“We’ve been put to the test in our games until now, and we’re still undefeated, so I’ve got to be happy,” he said.
Robyn Christie of Melrose said: “Recognition for Ayr, they’ve exploited their chances better than us.”
Grant Anderson's attempt in the 14th minute, which was increased by Frazier Climo, put Ayr in the lead. However, Melrose was able to let the numbers speak due to a yellow card for Scottish captain Rory Hughes, who had been released for the game by the Warriors, and Jason Baggot caught an unincreased attempt.
Climo extended Ayr's lead with a penalty kick before scoring a goal right at half-time and then single-handedly scoring an increased try, making it stand for Ayr at the break 17:5.
Melrose started the second half well, however, and Patrick Anderson's attempt, increased by Baggot, decreased the gap to five points.
Subsequently, there was a prolonged interruption due to a serious injury by Ruaridh Knott, who was carried off the pitch. After the sequel, Ayr extended his advantage through a attempt by Stafford McDowall increased by Climo.
Then Blair Macpherson, acting as captain of Ayr, received a yellow card, and again Melrose at the end of a period of enormous pressure had the extra man paid by a not-exaggerated attempt by Bruce Colvine.
However, the home team came backing back, and when Struan Hutchinson received a yellow card for Tackling without a ball against Climo, MacPherson scored a touchdown behind the advancing Ayr package from the penalty lane.
Climo increased as almost since the interruption, after Kyle Rowe intercepted David Armstrong's boxing kick and sent flanker Gregor Henry off for the home team's fifth attempt.
‘Still Game’ star seems ready for new career in gastronomy
“Still Game” star Ford Kieran appears to be ready for a move to the hospitality industry after it turned out he has been named as managing director of a licensed restaurant company.
The 56-year-old plays Jack Jarvis on the popular BBC show, whose screenplays he writes and in which he plays one of the starring roles together with his long-time comedy partner Greg Hemphill.
The two have announced the upcoming ninth season will be the last of the show, and Kiernan appears to be planning a life after Craiglang.
According to official registrations, he is managing director of Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor refused to commentate on the story, though a Scottish Sun source suggested Kiernan is interested in joining Glasgow's "thriving restaurant business."
“The sea belongs to us”: Bolivia enclosed by land hopes that a court will open its way to the Pacific
Sailors patrol in front of the naval headquarters in La Paz, decorated with thawl.
There is a sea-blue flag on public buildings.
Fleet bases from Lake Titicacase to Amazon bear the motto: “The sea is our rightful property.
Winning it back is a duty.”
Everywhere in Bolivia enclosed by land, the remembrance of the stretch of coast lost to Chile in a bloody conflict over resources in the 19th-century is still just as vivid as the desire to drive back across the Pacific.
These hopes may be as strong as they have been in decades, as Bolivia is awaiting a ruling by the International Court of Justice, which is due to be made on October 1 after five-year deliberations.
“Bolivia has the momentum, a spirit of unity and serenity and, of course, expects the outcome with a positive attitude,” said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Nationwide, many Bolivians will follow the ICJ's verdict on big screens, hoping that the court in The Hague will decide in favor of Bolivia's demand. According to this, Chile is committed to negotiating a sovereign access of Bolivia to the sea after decades of sporadic talks.
For Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic indigenous president, who faces a contentious battle for his re-election next year, there is also a lot at stake in Monday's verdict.
"We are on the verge of returning to the Pacific," he vowed at the end of August.
However, some analysts consider it unlikely that the court will decide in favor of Bolivia, and believe that little would change.
The U.N. body based in the Netherlands is not authorized to award Chilean territory and has stipulated that it will not decide on the outcome of possible talks.
Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert on international law, concludes that the ICJ's ruling is already six months after the final plea, according to Paz Zárate.
Far frequent from supporting Bolivia's case, the last four years could also mean a setback.
“The current Bolivian government has raised the issue of access to the sea,” Zárate said.
She hinted that Morales' belligerent rhetoric suffocated any remaining goodwill of Chile.
Bolivia and Chile will continue their talks at some point, but after these events, this becomes extremely difficult.
The two countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, dismissed the view that the court's decision had been made unusually quickly.
On Monday, Bolivia "has an extraordinary opportunity to usher in a new era of relations with Chile" and a chance to "put an end to the 139-year-long disputes for mutual benefit," he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales – still one of Latin America’s most popular presidents – was using the theme of the sea as a political crutch.
“Bolivia will never give up its right of access to the Pacific,” he added.
“Judgment is an opportunity to recognize that we must overcome the past.”
North Korea says nuclear disarmament will only take place if it can trust the U.S.
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho says his nation will never disarm its nuclear weapons first if it can't trust Washington.
Ri spoke before the United Nations General Assembly on Saturday.
He called on the United States to turn the promises made into action during a summit between the heads of state of rivals in Singapore.
His remarks come at a time when U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears to be on the verge of restartingling entangled nuclear diplomacy more than three months after summit with North Korean leader Kim Jong-un in Singapore.
Ri called the notion that further U.S. sanctions and opposition to a declaration to end the Korean War would ever bring the North to its knees as “brain fantasy.”
Washington is sceptical and does not want to agree to the declaration without Pyongyang taking substantial disarmament steps first.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second summit.
There is widespread skepticism, however, that Pyongyang is serious about abandoning an arsenal in which the country probably sees the only way to guarantee its security.
Pompeo is planning a visit to Pyongyang for next month to prepare for a second summit between Kim and Trump.
Fashion shows in Paris showcase the latest collections for huge headgear that will soon be available in the shopping streets near you
If you want to expand your hat collection or completely shield the sun, you won’t have to look any longer.
Designers Valentino and Thom Browne showed on the catwalk a palette of crazy oversized headgear for their 2019 summer collection that fascinated the fashion world at Paris Fashion Week.
Extremely impractical hats have flooded Instagram this summer, and these designers have sent their sensational creations across the catwalk.
Valentino's most outstanding piece was an extravagant beige hat with a spring-like wide rim that crushed the models' heads.
Other oversized accessories have been shown watermelons, a magician's hat and even pineapple, but these aren't intended to keep your head warm.
Thom Browne also unveiled a series of bizarre masks – just in time for Halloween.
Many of the colorful masks were provided with sewn-on lips and had more resemblance to Hannibal Lecter than with haute couture.
One creation resembled diving equipment, including snorkelling and diving goggles, while another looked like a molten ice cot.
And if you want to make another huge fashion statement, you're lucky.
Fashion observers predict the giant headgears could find their way into shopping streets near you.
The oversized hats follow “La Bomba”, the straw hat with 60 centimeters wide edge, which from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski everyone wears.
The cult label behind the extremely impractical hat that made big waves on social media sent another big creation across the catwalk: a beach bag made of straw that was almost as big as the model carrying a swimsuit that held the bag.
The bast bag in reddish orange with bast fringes and a white leather hendle was the outstanding piece of Jacquemus' summer 2019 collection "La Riviera" at the Paris Fashion Week.
Celebrity stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: "I expect large hats and beach bags to be available in the shopping streets for next summer. The designer has attracted so much attention that the demand for oversized accessories will hardly be ignorable."
John Edward: Language skills essential for global citizens
Scotland's independent schools continue to stand out for academic excellence. This has continued in 2018 with further outstanding examination results and is only underpinned by individual and joint achievements in the fields of sports, arts, music and other community ventures.
With more than 30,000 pupils from all over Scotland, these schools represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) strive to look after their pupils and their parents at the highest possible levels.
Independent schools aim to prepare their students for further education and higher education, the profession of their choice and their place as world citizens.
As an education sector that can design and put it into practice a tailor-made curriculum, we see that modern languages are still popular and sought-after in schools as electives.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you talk to someone in a language he understands, this is how you reach his head.
If you speak to him in his own language, you reach his heart.”
This is an impressive reminder that we cannot rely only on English if we are looking to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
From this year’s current examination results, we find that languages with the highest passing rates are at the top of the rankings of independent schools.
A total of 68 percent of the students who learned foreign languages scored grade A at the higher exams.
Data collected by 74 SCIS member schools showed that 72 percent of pupils in Mandarin scored grade A at the higher exams, while 72 percent of pupils who learned German also scored grade A, and 63 percent of pupils who learned Spanish also scored grade A.
This illustrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as an important knowledge that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Today, languages are regarded as electoral subjects as well as STEM subjects (natural sciences, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curricula of independent schools and in other places.
According to a survey by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014, 17 per cent of the reasons employers cited for the problems of filling vacancies were related to a lack of language skills.
Therefore, language proficiencies become increasingly imperative when it comes to preparing young people for their future profession.
As more and more vacancies presuppose languages, this knowledge is essential in a globalized world.
Regardless of the chosen profession, young people who have learned a second language will have a real advantage in the future through such a lifelong ability.
Being able to communicate directly with people from other countries will automatically give multilingual individuals an edge over competitors.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75 per cent could not speak a foreign language well enough to conduct a conversation, and French was spoken as the only language of a double-digit percentage (15 percent).
This is why it is important for today's children that we are now investing in language lessons.
Children who speak several languages, in particular languages from developing countries, will have better chances of finding meaningful employment.
In Scotland, different languages are taught at all schools.
A number of schools will focus on the more classical modern languages, while other languages will teach that are considered most important to the United Kingdom, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Wherever your child’s interests lie, there will always be a number of languages with correspondingly specialized teachers to choose from.
Scottish independent schools are committed to creating a learning environment that prepares the children and equips them with the skills necessary for their success, whatever the future.
At this stage in a global economic environment, it cannot be denied that languages continue to be of elementary importance for the future of the country. Therefore, this must be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should actually be regarded as “international communication skills.”
Independent schools will continue to offer young people in Scotland this choice, diversity and excellence.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is head of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools.
LeBron ahead of Lakers debut on Sunday in San Diego
For fans looking to see LeBron James competing for the Los Angeles Lakers for the first time, the wait will soon come to an end.
Lakers coach Luke Walton announced that James will play against the Denver Nuggets in San Diego on Sunday in the opening game of the pre-season.
However, how many minutes he will play has not yet been determined.
“It’s going to be more than one and less than 48,” Walton said on the Lakers’ official website.
Lakers reporter Mike Trudell tweeted that James is likely to only play a few minutes.
Earlier this week, James was asked about his plans for the Lakers' six-game pre-season schedule after training.
“I don’t need pre-season games at this stage of my career to prepare,” he said.
Time for Trump's rally in West Virginia
President Donald Trump begins a series of campaign rallies tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
This is the first of Trump's five rallies scheduled for next week, among others in well-minded places such as Tennessee and Mississippi.
Faced with hovering vote on confirming his election to the vacancy on the Supreme Court, Trump wants to build up support for November's imminent mid-term elections as Republicans run the risk of losing control of Congress in the process.
When's rally takes place in West Virginia tonight, and how can you watch online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for today, Saturday, September 29, 2018, at 7 p.m. (East Coast time).
You can follow Trump's rally in West Virginia below as a livestream on YouTube online.
Trump is likely to address the hearings of Supreme Court-nominated Brett Kavanaugh on accusations of sexual misconduct conducted this week in a tense atmosphere. The expected Senate vote on confirmation remains in limbo for up to a week while the FBI conducts investigations.
However, the main goal of this rally series is to support Republicans who will have to face off in a November-difficult election.
Therefore, President Trump's campaign team told Reuters that these five rallies next week serve to "mobilize volunteers and supporters as Republicans try to preserve and expand their majorities in the Senate and House of Representatives."
“Control over Congress is so important to his plans that the president will travel to as many states as possible, while the operational campaign season is approachinging,” a Trump campaign spokesperson told Reuters.
According to the West Virginia Metro News, supporters from “Ohio and Pensumen will be able to take part in Ohio and report media from Pittsburgh at the Wesbanco Arena in Wheeling this evening.
Saturday's visit is Trump's second visit this month to West Virginia, the state he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump is trying to base Republican Senate candidate from West Virginia, Patrick Morrisey.
"It's not a good sign for Morrisey that the president needs to come to try to boost him in the polls," Simon Haeder, a political scientist at West Virginia University, said, according to Reuters.
Ryder Cup 2018: U.S. team shows combat readiness to maintain hope for Sunday's individuals
After three one-sided rounds, Saturday afternoon's foursomes may have been exactly what this Ryder Cup needed.
The swinging pendulum of momentum is a pure invention as a sports concept, but one that players really believe in, especially in competitions like these.
So where do you think the momentum is now?
"They were in the lead with six points, and now it's four, so I think that's a little momentum for us," Jordan Spieth said.
Europe, of course, has an advantage and is four points ahead, while twelve more will follow.
However, according to Spieth, the Americans feel some wind in their sails and have reason to feel emboldened, not least the form of Spieth, who have played together throughout the day and can each boast three out of four points.
Spieth was incredibly strong from the tee to the green and leads by example.
The guttural screams of enthusiasm grew louder in the further course of his lap when he sank an important putt to decide the fourth match with four points for both, after he and Thomas had trailered after two holes with two points.
His putt, which decided the match on the 15th hole for them, triggered a similar cry that suggests the American team isn't out of the game yet.
“I really need to kneel and take care of my own game,” Spieth said.
That's all that's left for the individual players.
18 holes to make a mark.
Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood are the only players to have scored more points than Spieth and Thomas over the past two days, undoubtedly writing the history of the Ryder Cup.
Europe's unequal but sympathetic duo are at four out of four points and can't do anything wrong.
“Moliwood” wasn’t the only pair who hadn’t beaten a bogey on Saturday afternoon, but they had also avoided bogeys on Saturday morning, on Friday afternoon and at the back nine holes on Friday morning.
This series and the seemingly flowing energy between them and the exuberant crowd underpins that they are the players to beat on Sunday. Fleetwood and Molinari would be the most popular players to seal a possible European victory in the sun's setting over Le Golf National.
Preferably both at different holes at the same time.
To speak of European fame, however, would be premature.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson made short trials with Sergio Garcia, the morning's four-man hero, who played alongside Alex Noren.
A bogey and two doubles on the front nine holes put the Spaniard and the Swede in a clampdown they couldn't nearly overcome.
On Sunday, however, no one can help them out of trouble.
The foursomes and quadruple pairings are so fascinatingly up close because of the interactions between the couples, the advice they give or don't give, and the way a strategy can change lightningly.
The Europeans have played better as a team so far and are clearly in the lead on the last day. However, this round of four has also shown that the U.S. team is ready to fight, which some, especially in the USA, had doubted.
Europe takes the lead with 10:6 remaining on the last day of the Ryder Cup
Europe is 10:6 ahead after Saturday's fours and four-matches against the U.S. and goes into the final day of the Ryder Cup with a solid lead.
Ingenious duo Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari progressed with two wins over Tiger Woods, who struggled with difficulties and scored four points in Le Golf National.
The European squad led by Thomas Bjørn, which wants to win the trophy lost two years ago in Hazeltine, dominated in the fours in the morning against the flawed American squad and won the series with 3:1.
In the four-matches, the United States resisted more and won two matches, but were unable to catch up.
Jim Furyk's squad needed eight points in the twelve individuals played on Sunday to keep the trophy.
Fleetwood became the first European newcomer to gain four points in a row, while he and Molinari, christened “Molliwood” after a sensational weekend, only won four points as the second duo in the history of the Ryder Cup in their four opening matches.
After beating Woods and Patrick Reed in the fours, they harmonised superbly, beating the disenchanted Woods and American newcomer Bryson Dechambeau with an even more emphaticated 5&4.
Woods, who had dragged himself through two matches on Saturday, let his brilliance flash casually but has now lost 19 of his 29 matches in foursomes and foursomes and seven in a row.
Justin Rose, who had rested during the foursome in the mornning, returned and played the four-man games as Henrik Stenson's partner. They won 2&1 against Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, the world's number one and three.
For Europe, however, on this pleasant windy day southwest of Paris not everything went according to their ideas.
Three-time major winner Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas set the bar for the Americans with two points on Saturday.
They scored a tough 2&1 victory in the fours over Spain's Jon Rahm and Ian Poulter, beating Poulter and Rory McIlroy in the four-match 4&3 after losing the first two holes.
In the history of the Ryder Cup, it has only happened twice for a team to come back after going into the singles with a backlog of four points. As the title holder, however, for Furyk's squad a draw is enough to keep the trophy.
Having been second for two days, it doesn't look like they could launch a counter-attack on Sunday.
North Korad rejects unilateral disarmament without trust
North Korea's foreign minister told the United Nations on Saturday that continued sanctions deepened their distrust of the United States and the country would by no means unilaterally abandon its nuclear weapons under these circumstances.
Ri Yong Ho told the World Organization's annual General Assembly that North Korea had taken essential measures last year out of "good will." For example, it had stopped nuclear and missile tests, dismantled the nuclear test site and pledged not to multiply nuclear weapons and technologies.
"However, we do not see a corresponding reaction from the United States," he said.
“Without trust in the U.S., there’s no confidence in our national security, and under these circumstances we won’t unilaterally disarm ourselves first.”
While Ri reiterated North Korean complaints about Washington's resistance to a "phased" approach to denuclearization, where North Korea would be rewarded for successive steps, his statement seemed significant in that it did not reject unilateral denuclearization from the outset, as Pyongyang had done in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement by Kim Jong-un and Donald Trump at the first summit between an incumbent U.S. president and a North Korean leader on June 12 in Singapore. In which Kim had pledged to work towards the "denuclearization of the Korean peninsula," while Trump pledged guarantees for North Korea's security.
North Korea seeks an official end to the Korean War waged from 19550 to 1953, while the United States has said Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
In addition, Washington is resisting calls for an easing of strict international sanctions against North Korea.
“The U.S. insists on “denuclearization” and is increasing pressure from sanctions to achieve its goal through coercion, and are even opposed to “declaration of the end of the war,” Ri said.
“The assumption that sanctions can bring us to our knees is a fantasy of people who know nothing about us.
However, the problem is that the continued sanctions deepen our suspicion.”
Ri did not respond to plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump that the U.S. head of state had highlighted at the United Nations earlier this week.
Instead, the minister pointed to three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months, adding: “If South Korea were the other party on denuclearization instead of the U.S.”
Nevertheless, the tone of Ri's speech differed enormously from his speech last year, when he told the U.N. General Assembly it was inescapable to target with North Korea's missiles at the mainstant of the U.S. after "the evil President" Trump referred to Kim as a "missile man" on a suicide mission.
This year, Trump, who had threatened last year with the “complete destruction” of North Korea, showered Kim at the United Nations with praise for his courage in terms of disarmament. However, he said that there was still a lot of work to be done and the sanctions would have to remain in effect until the denuclearization of North Korea.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn't have a time frame for it: "Whether it takes two years, three years or five months - doesn't matter."
China and Russia argue that the U.N. Security Council must reward Pyongyang for the steps taken.
U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: “Enforcing the Security Council’s sanctions must definitely continue vigorously until we have reached complete, definitive and verified denuclearization.”
The Security Council has unanimously stepped up sanctions on North Korea since 2006 to financially dry up Pyongyang's nuclear weapons and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met with Ri on the sidelines of the U.N. General Assembly and subsequently said he would revisit Pyongyang next month to prepare a second summit.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip has not been successful.
In July, on departure from Pyongyang, he said progress had been made, only to be blamed hours later by North Korea for making "gangster-like demands."
North Korea vowed this month at a meeting with Moon to dismantle a missile base and a nuclear complex if the United States took "appropriate action."
He said Kim had told him that the intended "corresponding measures" were security guarantees Trump had given in Singapore, as well as steps to normalize relations with Washington.
Harvard students learn in a course to get enough to sleep
A new course at Harvard University ensures that all undergraduate students get more sleep this year. This is a measure against the spreading macho culture of keeping up with caffeine and learning throughout the night.
One scientist has found that students at the world's leading university are often unnecessary when it comes to paying attention.
The course was designed by Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, in his opinion, is the first such course in the U.S.
He was inspired to launch the course after giving a lecture on the effects of sleep deprivation on learning.
“At the end a girl came to me and asked, ‘Why am I only finding out about this now, in my last year?’
According to her words, no one had ever told her how important sleep is. That surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
This year’s first course explained to the students how the basics of good sleeping habits contribute to academic and athletic performance and also improve overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and managing director of the university's health services, said the university had opteded to introduce the course after it found that students suffered from severe sleep deprivation during the week.
The one-hour course includes a number of interactive assignments.
In one section, students see the image of a room in a dormitory and click on coffee cups, curtains, training shoes and books for information on the effects of caffeine and light, as well as the effects of lack of sleep on athletic performance and the importance of a sleep routine.
In another section, participants learn that long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes and cancer.
Subsequently, they are stimulated to think about their daily routine using a map of the university grounds with interactive symbols.
“We know it will not change students’ behavior immediately.
But we believe that they have the right to know this – just as you have the right to know how the decision to smoke will affect your health,” added Professor Czeisler.
The pride of pushing a “night shift” is still prevalent, he said, adding that modern technologies and the ever-increasing pressure on students made the lack of sleep a growing problem.
Ensuring enough good sleep, the “secret weapon” of all students should be against stress, exhaustion and fears, he said. In addition, weight gain could be avoided, as sleep deprivation puts the brain into hunger mode, so that they are constantly hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old student from California who studies chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Czeisler design the course. He had taken one of his courses last year in his first year at Harvard.
He said the course had opened his eyes and urged him to push for a university-wide course.
He hopes that in the next step, all postgraduate students will be asked to complete a similar degree programme before starting their studies at the competitive university.
According to Professor Czeisler’s recommendation, the students should set an alarm for the bedtime and wake-up and be aware of the harmful effects of the “blue light”, which emanates from electronic screens and LED lighting and can get the circadian rhythm out of the beat. This leads to problems when falling asleep.
Livingston vs Rangers 1:0: Menga goal defeats Gerrard's players
The Rangers lost another away game when Dolly Menga's hit for Steven Gerrard's disagreed squad meant a 0:1 defeat at Livingston.
The Ibrox squad wanted to record their first away win since the 4:1 triumph at St Johnstone, but Gary Holt's team added Gerrard's only second defeat in 18 games as manager, leaving his squad eight points behind those in the Ladbrokes Premiership by far.
Menga scored seven minutes before half-time, and the inspiration-poor Rangers didn't seem to have a chance to equalise.
While the Rangers now fall to sixth place, Livingston rises to third place and is only behind Hibernian in the goal difference.
The Rangers also face further trouble after line judge Calum Spence had to be treated because of a head wound by an object apparently thrown out of the guest block.
Gerrard made eight changes to the squad that overtook Ayr and reached the semi-finals of the Betfred Cup.
Holt, on the other hand, stayed with the same eleven Livingston players who took a point from the Hearts in the last week, and would have to be thrilled by how his well-trained squad kept their opponent under pressure at every turn.
The Rangers dominated possession, but Livingston achieved more when they had the ball.
They should have scored a goal after just two minutes when Menga's first levy Scott Pittman lead to Allan McGregor's goal. However, the midfielder missed his chance and scored next to it.
Then a long free-kick from Keaghan Jacobs reached captain Craig Halkett, but his defensive partner Alan Lithgow could only shoot past the back post.
The Rangers took control, but their game looked hopeful rather than convinced in the final third.
Alfredo Morelos was obviously of the opinion that after a quarter of an hour due to a clash between him and Steven Lawless he should have been given a penalty kick, but referee Steven Thomson did not let the Colombian's complaints apply.
The Rangers get only two shots on goal in the first half, but former Ibrox goalkeeper Liam Kelly had little trouble with Lassana Coulibaly's header and a tame shot from Ovie Ejaria.
Although Livingston's hit in the 34th minute may not correspond to the course of the game, there's no denying that they deserved him for their hard work alone.
Again, the Rangers couldn't deal with a ball scored by Jacobs in a standard situation.
Scott Arfield didn't react when Declan Gallagher shot the ball to Scott Robinson, who kept calm and picked Menga out for an easy finish.
Gerrard acted at half-time and exchanged Coulibaly for Ryan Kent. The change almost immediately took effect when the winger put Morelos in position, but the impressive Kelly raced forward from his line to block the ball.
Livingston, however, continued to get the guests to play exactly the game that suits them, with Lithgow and Halkett receiving one long ball after another.
Holt's squad could have extended their lead in the final phase, but McGregor stood up well and fended off Jacobs before Lithgow kicked a header over from the corner.
Rangers substitute Glenn Middleton also demanded a penalty kick late after a scuffle with Jacobs, but Thomson looked away again.
Almanach: The inventor of the Geiger counter
And now a page from our “Sunday Morning” almanach: On September 30, 1882, today 136 years ago, the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the device known as a Geiger counter.
The Geiger counter, which has since become the standard element of science, also became a standard element of pop culture, for example in the 1950 film “Bells of Coronado” with the seemingly unusual cowboy scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans.
“What in all the world is that?”
Rogers: “This is a Geiger counter that is used to search for radioactive minerals such as uranium.
If you put these headphones on, you can hear the effects of radioactive radiation in the minerals.”
Evans: “In fact, I’ll hear a cracking!”
“Hans” Geiger died in 1945, a few days before his 63rd birthday.
However, the invention that bears his name lives on.
New cancer vaccine may teach the immune system to “see” degenerate cells
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” degenerate cells and destroy them.
As part of treatment, the vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize degenerated cells.
In the method, immune cells are removed from a patient, which are altered in the laboratory.
These cells can “see” many cancers of common protein and are then injected again.
A test vaccine shows promising results in patients with various cancers.
Recognizing degenerate cells of a woman treated with the vaccine teaching the immune system to detect degenerate cells disappeared for more than 18 months.
In the method, immune cells are taken from a patient, which are altered in the laboratory in such a way that they can “see” the protein HER2 common many cancers, and the cells are then injected again.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, said, “Our results suggest that we have a very promising vaccine.”
HER2 “promotes the growth of various types of cancer”, including breast, ovarian, lung and colorectal cancers, as Professor Berzofsky explained.
A similar method in which patients are withdrawn immune cells and is taught to attack cancer cells has helped in the treatment of leukemia.
Kanye West released a pro-Trump tirade after appearinging at SNL, wearing a MAGA cap (“Make America Great Again”).
Things didn't go well.
Kanye West was booed on Saturday Night Live in the studio after widely praising U.S. President Donald Trump and said he wanted to run as a presidential nominee in 2020.
After sunging his third title of the evening, "Ghost Town," wearing a Make-America-Great-Again cap, he continued with a scolding cannonade against Democrats and reaffirminating his support for Trump.
“I’m asked so many times by whites, ‘How can you like Trump? He’s racist.’
So, if I was worried about racism, I would have left America a long time ago," he said.
SNL began the broadcast with a skit with Matt Damon in which the Hollywood star made fun of Brett Kavanaugh's testimony before the Senate Judiciary's Judiciary Committee on the accusations of sexual harassmented by Christine Blasey Ford.
Although the recording wasn't sent by West's scolding tirade, it was uploaded to social media by comedian Chris Rock.
It's not clear if Rock wanted to make fun of West with the release.
In addition, West complained to the audience that he had had difficulties backstage because of his headgear.
“They harassed me backstage.
They have said 'don't go out with the cap'.
You've bullied me!
And then they say I'm in a sunken area," he said, according to the Washington Examiner.
West continued, “Do you want to see the sunken area?” He said he was going to “put on my Supermancape, because that means you can’t tell me what I’ve had to do. Do you want it to go ahead with the world?
Try it with love.”
His remarks sparked at least twice booing calls from the audience, and SNL contributors seemed embarrassed, Variety reported. One person told the magazine: "It became dead-quient in the studio."
West had late been hired as a replacement for singer Ariana Grande, whose former friend, the rapper Mac Miller, had died a few days earlier.
West puzzled many with a talk of the title "I Love it," in which he was disguised as a Perrier bottle.
West received support from Candace Turner, the head of the conservative group TPUSA. She tweeted: “To one of the bravest spirits: THANK YOU HAVE OPPENED YOURSELVES TO THE MEASURE.”
The talk show presenter Karen Hunter, however, tweeted that West was simply himself and that was absolutely wonderful.
“But I don’t want to reward someone who, in my opinion, embraces an ideology harmful to my community and gives of itself (by buying their music or their clothes or supporting their ‘art’).
He's free.
The same applies to us,” she added.
Prior to the broadcast, the rapper had announced on Twitter that he had changed his name and was now “The being formally known as Kanye West”.
He's not the first artist to change his name, and follows in the footsteps of Diddy, who is also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
His rapper colleague Snoop Dogg beared the name Snoop Lion, and of course Prince, the deceased music legend, changed his name into a symbol and then into “The Artist Formerly Known As Prince.”
Prosecution charged with attempted murder in connection with stab injury at Belfast restaurant
A 45-year-old man has been charged with attempted homicide after a man was injured with a knife at a restaurant in eastern Belfast on Friday.
The incident took place in Ballyhackamore, according to police.
The defendant is due to appear before the Arbitration Tribunal in Belfast on Monday.
The allegations will be reviewed by the prosecutor's office.
'Game of Thrones' star Harington attacks toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is known for his role as Swirding Jon Snow in HBO's violent medieval fantasy series "Game of Thrones."
However, the 31-year-old actor has attacked the mecho hero's stereotype. He said such roles on the screen would mean that boys often felt they had to be strong in order to be respected.
Speaking to The Sunday Times Culture, Harington said he believed something had gone wrong and asked how to solve the problem of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo age.
Harington, who recently married Rose Leslie, also 31, who also participated in “Game of Thrones,” admitted that solving the problem was “very important.”
“Personally at the moment, I find the question of what went wrong with masculinity,” he said.
“What have we taught growing men about the problem we’re observing now?”
Harington believes television may be partly responsible for increasing toxic masculinity because of the very masculine characters.
He continued, “What is innate, and what is learned?
What is taught on television and on the street that makes boys feel like they have to embody this particular side of a man?
I think that's really one of the big questions of our time: how do we change that?
Something has clearly gone wrong with the young men.”
In addition, he admitted in the interview that he would not participate in prequels or sequels of “Game of Thrones” after the end of the series next summer. He had “enough of battlefields and horses.”
Starting in November, Harington can be seen in a reissue of Sam Shepard's "True West," the story of a film producer and his brother, a robber.
The actor recently revealed that for him the best thing about “Game of Thrones” that he had met his wife Rose.
“I met my wife on this show, so in this way I received my future family and my life from now on,” he said.
Rose Leslie starred in numerous Emmy's distinguished fantasy series Ygritte, the mistress of Harington's character Jon Snow.
The two got married at Leslie's family estate in Scotland in June 2018.
HIV/AIDS: China reports 14% increase in new cases
China has announced a 14°% increase in the number of citizens living with HIV or AIDS.
According to health officials, more than 820,000 people in the country are affected.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
Contrary to the past, in the vast majority of new cases, transmission took place through intercourse.
Until now, HIV spread rapidly in some parts of China as a result of infected blood transfusions.
However, the number of people infected with HIV in this way has been reduced to almost zero, Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
Year-on-year, however, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS has risen by 100,000 in China.
HIV transmission through intercourse is an acute problem in China's LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against members of the LGBT community is said to be widespread.
Due to the country's conservative values, studies have estimated that 70-90% of men who have intercourse with men ultimately marry women.
Many of the disease transmissions are due to insufficient protection during intercourse in these relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has been promising, among other things, universal access to HIV drugs to address the problem.
Maxine Waters denies employee leaking GOP senators' data, criticizing 'dangerous lies' and 'conspiracy theories'
Maxine Waters, a lawmaker in the U.S. House of Representatives, disputed claims on Saturday that a member of her staff had released the personal information of three Republican U.S. senators on the lawmakers.
The Democrat from Los Angeles argued that the claims of “ultra-righted” experts and websites would be disseminated.
"Lies, lies and even more abhorrent lies," Waters tweeted.
The published information is said to include the private addresses and phone numbers of U.S. senators Lindsey Graham from South Carolina, as well as Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
The information surfaced online on Thursday. They were released during the hearing of a Senate committee on the accusations made against Supreme Court Candidate Brett Kavanaugh by an unidentified person in the government district.
The release took place after the three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
Conservative websites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that the IP address that identifies the source of the posts was assigned to Waters' office and published the information of a member of Waters' personnel, The Hill reported.
“This unfounded allegation is completely false and an absolute lie,” Waters continued.
“The member of my staff—whose identity, personal information, and security were jeopardized by these fraudulent and false accusations—were in no way responsible for leaking this information.
This unfounded allegation is completely false and an absolute lie.”
Waters' statement quickly sparked criticism online, including that of Ari Fleischer, a former White House press secretary.
“This denial is furious,” Fleischer wrote.
“This suggests she doesn’t have the right temperament to be a member of Congress.
Individuals who are accused of something they didn't don't get angry.
You must not be defiant.
You must not question the motives of the accuser.
You have to be calm and serene.”
Fleischer seemed to compare Waters' reaction to Democrats' criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, who has been accused by critics of appearing too angry during Thursday's hearing.
Omar Navarro, a Republican nominee who is competing in the mid-term elections to replace Waters, also expressed his thoughts on Twitter.
"Big deal if that's true," he tweeted.
Waters said in her statement that her office had informed the relevant authorities and law enforcement about these fraudulent allegations.
"We will ensure that the perpetrators are revealed," she continued, "and that they will be held accountable for all their actions that are destructive and dangerous for all members of my staff."
“Johnny English – You Only Live Three Times” – Weak Spy parody with Rowan Atkinson
It is now a tradition to look for Brexit references in every British-coloured new film. This also seems to apply to this revival of the action comedy parody franchise “Johnny English”, which began in 2003 with “Johnny English – The spy who siftened it” stubberingly revived in 2011 with “Johnny English”.
Is an ironic self-parody on the subject, how obviously stupid we are, becoming a new export opportunity for the nation?
In any case, the license of the bright-eyed, grimacing and incompetent Johnny English has been extended for the second time to mess up. His name signals more than anything else that he is a general comic creation for moviegoers in the non-English-speaking world.
Of course, he’s once again the dungy secret agent who bizarrely considers himself cool and cosmopolitan, while in reality he’s made up some clouseau, a pinch of Mr. Bean, and a gossip of this guy who, at the opening ceremony of the 2012 Olympics in London, contributed a single note to the title tune of “The Hour of the Winner.”
In addition, the character was originally based on the chaos-behind travelers and international secret agents that Atkinson played in the now-forgotten Barclaycard television commercials.
The last adventure of Johnny English has one or two nice moments.
I liked how Johnny English approached a helicopter in a medieval armor and the rotor bladdes briefly applied to his helmet.
Atkinson's talent for physical comedy emerges, but the humour seems quite weak and strangely superfluous, especially because the "serious" film series such as "007" and "Mission Impossible" now confidently offer comedic elements.
The humour seems to be aimed more to children than to adults, and for me, Johnny English's crazy misfortunes aren't as imaginative and focused as Atkinson's silent movie gags in the role of Bean.
The eternally current premise now is that Britain is in great danger.
A cyberhacker has infiltrated the super-secret British spy network and revealed the identities of all British agents in action, much to the displeasure of the agent on duty, a regrettably small role for Kevin Eldon.
This is the last straw for the prime minister, an inflated and afflicted figure who is already facing complete collapse due to her political unpopularity: Emma Thompson is doing her best with this quasi-Teresa-May character, but the script doesn't give much to work with
Her intelligence advisers inform her that all active spies have been unmasked and she must therefore get someone out of retirement.
This also includes the clumsy Johnny English himself, who now works as a teacher on a chic institution, but unofficially gives lessons while working as an undercover agent. There are a few gags, because English offers a spy academy reminiscent of “School of Rock”.
English will be taken back to Whitehall for an emergency meeting and reunited with its former heavily audited Kumpan Bough, played again by Ben Miller.
Bough is now married to a submarine commander, an annoying woman of higher society in whose role Vicki Pepperdine is somewhat squandered.
So Batman and Robin, who are always terribly wrong in Her Majesty's secret service, are back in action, encountering Olga Kurylenko's beautiful femme Fatale Ophelia Bulletova.
In the meantime, the Prime Minister is dangerously under the spell of a charismatic tech billionaire, Jake Lacy's sinister Jason Volta, who claims to be able to solve Britain's computer problems.
English and Bough begin their odyssey of grotesque jokes: dressed as waiters, they set a fancy French restaurant on fire, causing chaos as they smuggle themselves aboard Volta's luxury yacht, and English triggers pure anarchy as he tries to familiarize himself with the help of a virtual reality headset with the interior of Volta's house.
In this last sequence all registers are drawn, but as sympathetic and funny as it is, the topic is already discussed in detail on children's television.
Quite moderate fabric.
As with the other Johnny English films, I had to ask myself, “Can’t the British film industry Rowan Atkinson play a role that truly lives up to his talent?”
Labour denies working on a plan under which Britons work four days a week but are to be paid for five days
Jeremy Corbyn's Labour Party is to be considering a radical plan that stipulates that Britons work four days a week but be paid for five days.
The party reportedly wants company leaders to pass on the savings achieved by the AI revolution (artificial intelligence) to employees in the form of an additional day off.
With this, the employees would have a three-day weekend, but would still receive the equal pay.
According to sources, the idea “fits” with the party’s economic agenda and its plans to shift balance in the country in favor of workers.
The transition to a four-day week was advocated by the Trades Union Constregation as an opportunity for workers to take advantage of the changing economy.
A high-ranking source from the Labour Party told the Sunday Times: "A policy review is expected to be announced before the end of the year.
“A change will not occur overnight, but a four-day working week is an aspiration consistent with the party’s approach to restoring the economic balance in favour of workers, as well as with the party’s general industrial policy strategy.”
The Labour Party wouldn't be the first party to advocate such an idea. Already the Green Party promised the launch of a 4-day week during the 2017 general election campaign.
However, the approach is currently not endorsed by the Labour Party as a whole.
A Labour Party spokesperson said: "A 4-day week is not party politics and it will not be considered by the party."
Shadow chancellor John McDonnell clarified his notion of a socialist revolution in the economy at the Labour conference last week.
McDonnell said he was determined to regain the power of “anonymous directors” and “profit makers” in public utilities.
The shadow chancellor's plans also mean that the current shareholders of the water utilities may not get their entire share back, as a Labour government could make "deductions" due to alleged misconduct.
He also confirmed the corresponding plans to appoint workers to the boards of companies and set up Inclusive Ownership Funds to hand over 10% of the equity of private companies to employees who collect annual dividends of up to £500.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy speak out in 60 Minutes news magazine whether the FBI's Investigation in the Kavanaugh Case
The FBI investigation into the allegations against Judge Brett Kavanaugh has postponed a final vote on his appointment to the Supreme Court by at least a week and raises the question of whether the agency's results could affect Republican senators to gain their support.
In an interview on Sunday, 60 Minutes correspondent Scott Pelley questioned Republican senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham about whether the FBI could reveal anything that could ultimately change their mind.
Kennedy seemed to be more open than his colleague from South Carolina.
"I mean, of course," Kennedy said.
“I communicated at the trial that I spoke with Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him afterwards, this claim was made, and asked if he did it.
He was determined, determined and unequivocal.”
Graham's voice, however, appears to be carved in stone.
“My opinion is clear about Brett Kavanaugh, and there would have to break a bomb,” he said.
“Dr. Ford, I don’t know what happened, but I know this: Brett vehemently disputed this,” Graham added, referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
“And all who called them could not confirm it.”
It's 36 years now.
And I can't see any changes."
What is the Global Citizen Festival and was helped to mitigate poverty as part of this festival?
This Saturday, the Global Civic Festival will take place in New York, an annual music festival with an impressive lineup of stars and an equally impressive mission – to fight poverty in the world.
Tens of thousands will attend the Global Citizen Festival, which is taking place for the 7th time this year, to witness not only the performances of Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the event's real goal: fighting extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, which first took place in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project. As part of this project, an international interest group seeks to fight poverty by motivating more and more people to actively engage in poverty alleviation.
To receive a free ticket for the event (the other option would be to buy a VIP ticket), you need to complete a number of tasks. These include actions such as volunteering, communicating with a leading politician worldwide (by email or phone) or similar measures to raise awareness of the goal – fighting poverty.
How successful is the project when you consider that Global Citizen will remain 12 years to achieve the goal?
Is the idea of promising a cool free concert a sensible way to convince others that it’s time to take action, or just another case of so-called clicktivism – if you think you’re making a difference by signing an online petition or sending a tweet?
Global Civic announced that the project has recorded more than 19 million actions from its supporters since 2011 and achieved various goals.
These actions are said to have helped encourage leaders around the world to announcing more than $37 billion worth commitments and strategies that will affect the lives of more than €2.25 billion by 2030.
In early 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and announcements arising from its actions, at least $10 billion of which has already been disbursed or raised via fundraising.
According to the group's estimates, secured funds have so far had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
Some of the most important commitments include The Power of Nutrition project, a partnership between investors and implementers based in the United Kingdom who have pledged to support children in their optimal development, and who have pledged to help Rwanda with €35 million to combat malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens.
“With the support of the United Kingdom government, donors, national governments and global citizens like you, we can make the social injustice of malnutrition a footnote in history,” the ambassador of The Power of Nutrition, Tracey Ullman, told audiences during a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that after more than 5,000 actions urging Britain to improve the nutritional situation of mothers and children, the government announced the funding of the The Power of Nutrition project, which aims to reach 5 million women and children and provides nutritional assistance.
In response to one of the frequently asked questions on the website, namely the question of what are the reasons why Global Citizen can combat extreme poverty,
They replied, “It will be a long and exhausting road with ups and downs.
But much like the big civil rights and anti-apartheid movements ahead of us, we will succeed as we are stronger and more influential together.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are among the artists performing at this year's event in New York. Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman are also presenters.
U.S. could use the navy for blockade purposes to obstruct Russian energy export – Interior Ministry
According to Washington Examine, Washington can resort to the navy if necessary so that energy from Russia does not reach the markets including the Middle East, U.S. Secretary of Stemer Ryan Zinke said.
Zinke asserted that Russia's deployment in Syria is a pretext to explore new energy markets. Russia is currently operating in these areas at the invitation of the rightful government.
“I think the real reason for Russia’s use in the Middle East is the negotiation of negotiating new sales markets in the field of energy, just like in Eastern Europe,” he said.
And according to the official, there are means and means to tackle this.
“The United States, together with the navy, can ensure that the seaways remain open and block them if necessary to ensure that the energy from Russia doesn’t reach the market,” he said.
Zinke addressed participants in the Consumer Energy Alliance event, a non-profit group that sees itself as a “voice of electricity consumers” in the U.S.
He drew a comparison between Washington's approaches to dealing with Russia and Iran, noting that they are virtually identical.
“Using and replacing fuels is more or less the economic option in Iran and Russia,” he said, while describing Russia as a “one trick pony” with an economy dependent on fossil fuels.
The declarations were made when the Trump administration was about to expand the export of liquefied natural gas to Europe, thereby replacing Russia, the far more cost-effective option for consumers in Europe.
To this end, government officials, including U.S. President Donald Trump himself, are trying to convince Germany to withdraw from the “inappropriate” Nord Stream 2 pipeline, with which, according to Trump, Berlin became the “prisoner” of Moscow.
Moscow has stressed on several occasions that the €11 billion Nord Stream 2 pipeline, which is expected to double the existing pipeline capacity to 110 billion cubic meters, is an exclusively economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington's massive opposition to the project is attributable to economic reasons and is an example of unfair competition.
“I think we are all of the opinion that energy should not be used as a means to exert pressure, and that consumers can choose the suppliers themselves,” Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said after a meeting with U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry in Moscow in September.
The U.S. has received headwinds from Germany, which has confirmed its commitment to the project.
Germany's leading industrial organisation, the Federation of German Industries (BDI), has called on the US to stay out of EU energy policy and bilateral treaties between Berlin and Moscow.
“It is a big problem when a third country interferes in our energy supply,” the board of the BDI Dieter Kempf said after a meeting between Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warren wants to "clearly consider" a 2020 presidential nominee
Senator for Massachusetts Elizabeth Warren announced on Saturday that she would “exactly examine” a presidential candidacy after the midterm elections.
During a town hall meeting in Holyoke, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed she was considering a candidacy.
According to The Hill, Warren said it would be time for women to go to go to Washington and repair our broken government, and that includes women at the top.
Warren plans to “examine closely” a presidential candidacy after November 6.
Warren said during the meeting that President Trump is steering this country in the wrong direction.
"I'm extremely concerned about how Donald Trump deals with our democracy," she said.
Warren hasn't taken a leaf in her criticism of Donald Trump and Brett Kavanaugh, candidate for the Supreme Court.
In a tweet on Friday, she announced that prior to the vote, of course, an FBI investigation is needed.
However, a poll released Thursday showed that a majority of Warren's electorate doesn't think it should run for 2020.
According to Suffolk University Political Research Center and Boston Globe poll, 58 percent of potential voters in Massachusetts said the senator shouldn't run.
32 percent of voters backed such a candidacy.
The poll showed more support for a candidacy of former Governor Deval Patrick; with 38 percent favoring potential candidacy, and 48 per cent opposed.
The other names of prominent Democrats who are eligible with regard to a possible 2020 candidacy include former Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont's senator Bernie Sanders.
According to Associated Press, Biden shared that he would officially decide by January.
Sarah Palin expresses herself at the Donald Trump ralley on post-traumatic stress disorder which Track Palin suffers
Track Palin, 26, had been in Iraq for a year.
He was arrested and charged with domestic violence Monday night.
"What my son's going through, what he's going through when he comes back, it's all other families who feel the effects of PTSD and some of the wounds that our soldiers are returning with," she shared to the audience at a ralley for Donald Trump in Tulsa, Oklahoma.
Palin referred to his arrest as an "elephant in the room" and said about her son and other war veterans that they are coming back changed, that they are coming back and wondering if there is this respect for what other soldiers and pilots and every other member of the military have given to the country.
He was arrested Monday in Wasilla, Alaska, and charged with domestic violence against a woman, according to Dan Bennett, a spokesman for Wasilla's Police Department.
18 states and D.C. support a remedy for the new asylum policy
18 states and the District of Columbia support an appeal on the new U.S. policy that deniates asylum to victims of gang activities or domestic violence.
Representatives of the 18 states and the district filed a court expert statement Friday in Washington to support an asylum seeker questioning the policy, NBC News reported.
The plaintiff's full name in the Grace-against Sessions lawsuit filed by the American Civil Liberties Union against federal policy in August has not been disclosed.
She shared that her partner “and his violent gang sons” abused them, but U.S. officials declined their asylum application on July 20.
She was arrested in Texas.
Prosecutors who support Grace referred to El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, where very many people make an application for asylum in the U.S., as nations struggling with major problems with criminal gangs and domestic violence.
The new U.S. asylum policy overturned a decision by the Board of Immigrant Appeals of 2014 that allowed illegal immigrants fleeing domestic violence to seek asylum.
The attorney general of the District of Columbia, Karl Racine, said in a statement Friday that the new policy "ignorates decades of legislation at the state, state and international levels."
“The federal law stipulates that all asylum applications be judged based on the respective facts and circumstances of the application, and this court violates this principle,” was stated in the court expert statement.
Furthermore, the lawyers argued in the statement that the policy that denies immigrant access to the country has an unfavorable effect on the economy in the U.S., as the immigrants could rather operate as entrepreneurs and provide the necessary workers.
Attorney General Jeff Sessions ordered in June that immigratory judges no longer grant asylum to victims fleeing domestic violence and the violencence of criminal gangs.
“Asylum exists for all those who leave their home country because they are persecuted or out of fear because of race, religion, nationality, or belonging to a particular social group or political persuasion,” Sessions said in his June 11 policy announcement.
It has never been the plan to defuse all problems by granting asylum, even all the serious problems that people struggle with every day worldwide.
Desperate rescue attempts in Palu as the death toll in the run doubles over time
For the survivors, the situation was getting worse.
“It’s all very tense,” says 35-year-old mother Risa Kusuma, reassuring her little fevering son in a rescue center in Palu’s shattered city centre.
“Every minute comes a new ambulance with the dead.
Clean drinking water is scarce.”
Inhabitants returned to their ruined houses to collect their soaked belongings, recovering everything they could find.
Hundreds were injured, and employees of the hospitals damaged by the magnitude 7.5 earthquake were completely overloaded.
Some of the injured, including Dwi Haris, who contracted vertebral fractures and a broken shoulder, remained outside Palu's military hospital, where patients in the outdoor area were being treated for the ongoing severe aftershocks.
With tears in his eyes, he recounted how the violent earthquake shook the fifth-floor hotel room he shared with his wife and daughter.
“Everything went way too fast.
I think I were pinched under the invaded walls," he told the Associated Press, adding that the reason for staying in town was a wedding.
“I heard my wife’s calls for help, but then there was rest.
I don't know what happened to my wife and my child.
I can only hope that they are in a safe place.”
U.S. ambassador accuses China of tyranny with 'propaganda displays'
A week after an official Chinese newspaper launched a four-page ad in an American daily announcing the mutual benefits of trade between the U.S. and China, the U.S. ambassador to China accused the country's capital Beijing of exploiting the American press for progaganda purposes.
U.S. Donald Trump last Wednesday referred to the China Daily's paid side in the Des Moine's Register, the best-selling magazine in Iowa after accusing China of interfering in the U.S. congressional election on Nov. 6, an indictment denying China.
Trump's allegations that Beijing has attempted to interfere in the U.S. election is in line with U.S. officials' notification to Reuters; a new phase of an escalating campaign sparked by Washington to put pressure on China.
While it is common for foreign governments to run advertisements to promote the economy, Beijing and Washington are currently engaged in a sprawling trade war, with tariffs on imports for both countries.
Chinese retaliatory tariffs at the start of the trade war were supposed to hit exporters in states such as Iowa that supported Trump's Republican Party, Chinese and U.S. experts said.
Terry Branstad, U.S. ambassador to China and former long-time governor of Iowa, which exports agricultural products to China on a large scale, said Beijing has harmed workers, owners of farms and businesses.
China, Branstad said in Sunday's Des Moine's register, is now doubling this tyranny by placing propaganda ads in its own free press in the U.S.
“By disseminating its propaganda, the Chinese government is claiming the tradition of free speech and the independent press in America by placing paid advertising in the Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
“By contrast, you’ll only find at the newspaper kiosks here in Beijing in a limited number of opposing voices, and you won’t see any real reflection of the differing opinions the Chinese could have about China’s disturbing economic development, given that the media is firmly in the hands of China’s Communist Party,” he wrote.
He added that one of China's best-known newspapers did not accept the offer to publish his article, but he did not mention the newspaper's name.
Republicans denounce female voters ahead of midterm elections with the debacle surrounding Kavanaugh, observers warn
Since many of the most influential Republicans support and defend the Supreme Court candidate, Brett Kavanaugh, in the face of multiple accusations of sexual violence, observers have warned of a backlash during the upcoming midterm elections, which could emanate from women in particular.
The mood in this context was extremely tense, and most Republicans have already reported that they are proceeding with a vote.
“You can’t undo things like that,” said Grant Reeher, professor of political science at Syracuse University’s Maxwell Syracuse University The Hill for an article published on Saturday.
Reher said that he doubts Senator Jeff Flake's (R-Arizona) last-minute push was sufficient for an FBI investigation to appease disgruntled voters.
“Women don’t forget what was yesterday, they don’t forget it tomorrow and also in November,” Karine Jean-Pierre, first adviser and national spokeswoman for the progressive group MoveOn said on Friday.
On Friday morning, protesters shouted "November is coming!" when Republicans who control the Judiciary Committee decided to detain Kavanaugh's nomination despite Dr. Christine Blasey Ford's statement.
Democratic enthusiasm and motivation are an extremely important issue, said Stu Rothenberg, an impartial political observer of the news site.
“People say it’s already high, that’s true.
But it could be higher, especially among women voters in the suburbs and among younger voters, people between 18 and 29 who, although they don’t like the president, often don’t vote.”
Even before Ford's public testimony, which detailed their allegations of sexual assault against the Supreme Court candidate, observers hinted that a backlash could be the consequence if Republicans advance confirmation.
"This situation is a confused mess for the GOP," Michael Steele, former chairman of the Republican National Committee, told NBC News at the beginning of last week.
"It's not just about the panel's vote, the final vote, or the review of Kavanaugh; it's also about the way Republicans dealt with it and how they've handled it," Guy Cecil, director at Priorities USA, a group that supports the election of Democrats, told the news channel.
However, Americans seem to disagree with whom they are supposed to believe because of Ford and Kavanaugh's statements, with a slightly larger share on the side of the latter.
A new YouGov survey showed that 41% of respondents were definitely or probably convinced of Ford's statement, while 35% said they definitively or probably believed Kavanaugh.
Furthermore, 38% said they think Kavanaugh probably or definitely lied with his testimony, while 30% reported the same about Ford.
Flake's pressure, the FBI is currently investigating the accusations put forward by Ford and at least one other plaintiff, Deborah Ramirez
Ford issued an affidavit last week before the Senate Judiciary Committee that she was assaulted by drunken Kavanaugh at the age of 17.
Ramirez maintains that the Supreme Court nominee had exposed his genitals to her when they were at a party during her studies in Yale in the 1980s.
Inventor of the World Wide Web plans to launch a new internet to oppose Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is currently launching a startup with the aim of establishing a competition with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The technology legend's latest project, Inrupt, is a company based on Berners-Lee's open-source Solid platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data will be stored and who will have access to which information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee stated that the intention behind Inrupt was “world domination”.
"We have to take action now," he said on the topic of startup.
"It's a historic moment."
The app deploys Solid’s technology so that users can create their own personal online data store or POD.
It can include contact lists, task lists, a calendar, a music library, and other tools for the private and corporate sectors.
Similar to Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify, all content is available in one browser and simultaneously.
The special thing about the personal online data store is that the user alone can decide who can access which data.
The company describes this concept as personal reinforcement and development over data.
According to CEO of John Bruce, the idea behind Inrupt is that the company uses resources, processes, and appropriate capabilities so that Solid is available to everyone.
The company currently includes Berners-Lee, Bruce, some developers hired to work on this project, as well as a community of volunteer programmers, and an IBM-acquainted security platform.
Beginning this week, technology developers worldwide will be able to create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee announced that he and his team will not engage with Facebook and Google on whether to make a sweeping change that completely reshapes their entire business models overnight.
“We are not asking for their permission.”
In a post on Medium published on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt has the task of providing commercial energy generation and an ecosystem to protect the integrity and quality of the new solid-based web.
In 1994, Berners-Lee redesigned the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months Berners-Lee has been instrumental in the debate on net neutrality.
Even with the launch of Inrupt, Berners-Lee remains the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
“I’m extremely confident about this new era of the web,” he added.
Priest Bernard Vann, bearer of the Victoria Cross in World War 1, was honoured
The only clergyman of the Church of England who was awarded the Victorian Cross during World War I was honoured 100 years later in his hometown.
Leutenant colonel and priest Bernard Vann won the award due to his use in the attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt on September 29, 1918.
However, he was killed by a sniper four days later and has never learned that he had won Britain's highest war award.
His two grandchildren unveiled a memorial stone on Sunday at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of the grandchildren, Michael Vann, said that this act was full of symbolic power, since the memorial stone was unveiled exactly 100 years after the heroic act for which his grandfather was honoured.
According to The London Gazette, Lieutenant Colonel Vann led his battalion across the Canal de Saint-Quentin on September 29, 1918; through dense fog and under heavy shelling of field cannons and machine guns.
He later penetrated the line of fire and, with “the greatest bravery,” led the line forward ahead with a field weapon alone and knocked out three of the military units.
Lieutenant Colonel Vann was shoveled on October 4, 19198 by a German sniper; just over a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, 72, said his grandfather's actions were something he could never live up to, and at the same time something that makes you humble.
Together with his brother Dr. James Vann, he laid a wreath after the parade under the direction of the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann stated that he was very honored to participate in the parade and added that the bravery of a real hero became visible above all through the support of many people.
MMA fans have stayed up all night to watch Bellator 206, instead there was Peppa Pig
Imagine you've stayed up all night looking at an exciting Bellator 206, and then you won't be able to watch the main event.
San Jose's bill contained 13 fights, including 6 on the main map, and was broadcast live all night in the United Kingdom on Channel 5.
At 6 a.m. then, just as Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing for their fight, viewers in the United Kingdom were not badly amazed when Peppa Pig was suddenly seen.
Some remained untouched after staying awake until the early hours of the morning to specifically see the fight.
One fan on Twitter described the move to the cartoon character as a kind of bad joke.
"Due to a regulation by the government, this content is classified as non-adolescent-free, so at 6 o'clock to a children's show had to be switched," said Dave Schwartz, senior vice president in marketing and communications at Bellator when intervieweed about the broadcast.
“Peppa, the pig, exactly.”
Bellator’s board of directors Scott Coker announced that the company will work on the schedule to accommodate viewers from the United Kingdom in the future.
“I think that the problem could already be solved in the repeat game,” he said.
“But it’s 6 a.m. on a Sunday with viewers in the United Kingdom, and we won’t resolve the incident until Sunday our time, Monday local time there.
But we're sticking.
There was quite a lot of communication during the change. It was quite confused and it wasn’t always nice.
We tried to solve the problem, and have assumed that it was a technical fault.
What was not the case, it was a problem on the part of the government.
I promise it won't happen again.
We’re going to reduce it all to 5 instead of 6 fights, as usual, and we’ve tried to provide the fans with the best possible experiences.
The whole situation is quite unfortunate.”
Deserst Island Discs: Tom Daley feels 'inferior' on the subject of sexuality
Tom Daley, who has competed as a water jumper at various Olympic Games, says he grew up with the feeling of being inferior to everyone else's because of his sexuality. But this feeling had motivated him to become so successful.
The 24-year-old said that he had only realized at secondary school that he was different from others.
On the first Radio 4 Desert Island discs with Lauren Laverne, he said that he spoke out on the subject of gay rights to encourage others.
He also meant that since he became a father, the victories at the Olympics are no longer so important for him.
The actual presenter of the long-term show, Kirsty Young, pauses for a few months due to illness.
On Laverne's first broadcast, he said he used to consider himself inferior in comparison to others, as love for boys and girls was socially unacceptable.
“Until today, the feeling of being inferior to others and different from others is reason for my power and strength and ultimately for my success.”
He wanted to prove that he is a very special personality to not disappoint anyone when his sexual orientation became known.
The two-time bronze medal winner at the Olympics is now a prominent LGBT activist and used his appearance at this year's Commonwealth Games in Australia to advance the decriminalization of homosexuality in other countries.
He said he had commented on this issue because fortunately he can live without restrictions and without discrimination and would like to give courage to others.
The three-time world champion fell in love with U.S. filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013. The realization of falling in love with a man was an absolute surprise for him.
Daley married the 20-year-older Oscar winner the previous year, but he shared that the age difference has never been an issue.
He thinks that if you had to go through so much as a child and adolescent – he took part in the Olympics for the first time at 14 and his father died of cancer three years later, it’s hard to find someone of the same age with similar experiences.
The couple have had a son since June bearing the name Robert Ray Black-Daley, and Daley says his entire point of view has changed since then.
Last year, everything would have revolved around gold medals.
But for him, there are now more important things than Olympic gold medals.
His son Robbie is now his main prize.
He bears the same name as his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said that his father did not accept the approaching death, and one of the last things he asked for was the tickets for London 2012, as he wanted to be at the forefront.
“I couldn’t tell him that he wouldn’t be there anymore,” he said.
"I held his hand when he stopped breathing, and it was only after that I finally realized that he is not invincible," he said.
The following year, Daley won the bronze medal at the 2012 Olympics.
“I just knew it’s always been my dream to dive in front of a home crowd at Olympics – that’s amazing,” he said.
His first song selection – Proud by Heather Small – was also influenced by this. He listened to the song in the run-up to the Olympic Games and still caused goosebumps.
Desert Island discs on BBBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15 BST.
Mickelson, who isn't in form right now, sits on the substitutes" bench at Saturday's Ryder Cup
American Phil Mickelson will set a record at his 47th Ryder Cup game on Sunday, but he has to make great efforts so that the whole thing doesn't become a debacle.
Mickelson, who is playing at the event for the 12th time, was sent to the substitutes" bench by captain Jim Furyk for the Fourballs and Foursomes on Saturday.
Instead of being at the heart of the action, as usual, the five-time major winner spent his day cheering on others and working on his game, hoping to fix the problems that burden him.
Even at the peak of his career, 48-year-old Mickelson has never been the most direct golfer and thus not an ideal line-up for the narrow Le Golf National golf course, where misguided balls are regularly punished.
And as if the pitch itself wasn't discouraging enough, Mickelson will face perfectionist British Open champion Francesco Molinari in Sunday's ninth game, who has teamed up with Tommy Fleetwood, newcomer at those tournaments to win all four games this week.
If the Americans put down a hot start with four points behind in the 12 singles matches, Mickelson's game could prove to be absolutely crucial.
Furyk expressed confidence about Mickelson and couldn't add much to that either.
"He was completely aware of his role today, gave me a slap on the back and put his arm around me and said he was ready for tomorrow," Furyk said.
"He's got a lot of self-confidence.
He is one of the world's well-known personalities and he has done so much for these teams in the past and this week.
I didn't think he'd be at two games.
I had imagined more, but that's how it's gone and that's what we expected.
Like everyone else, he wants to be there.”
Mickelson will reach Nick Faldo's record for the most Ryder Cup matches played on Sunday.
It could mean the end of a Ryder Cup career that never quite matched the highlights of his personal record.
Mickelson can record 18 wins, suffered 20 defeats and reached seven halves, although Furyk announced that his presence alone enriched the team.
“He’s funny and sarcastic, original and likes to make some fun, and he’s a great person who’s popular with the whole team,” he explained.
"I think the younger players were happy to get to know him a little better this week. It was fun to watch them.
He's a really interesting person who gives a lot of impetus."
Captain Thomas Bjørn knows it may soon be over with the lead
Thomas Bjørn, captain of the European team, knows from experience that a big lead, leading into the final singles matches in the Ryder Cup, can quickly become an unpleasant situation.
The Dane debuted in Valderrama in 1997, where a team led by Seve Ballesteros had a five-point lead over the Americans, but only came just over the finish line and took the lead with the smallest possible lead and in the process achieved a 141⁄2–131⁄2 victory.
“The thought remains that we had a big lead in Valderrama, we had a big lead in Brookline, where we lost, and in Valderrama, where we won, but only narrowly,” said Bjørn (pictured in the photo) after seeing the victory 5–3 of the Class of 2018 on both Friday and yesteryear to lead 10-6 at Le Golf National.
The experience shows me and all the team members that it's not over.
It'll really start tomorrow.
Lie down and do just the right thing.
The whole thing isn't over until the dots are on the scoreboard.
We have a goal. We want to win this trophy, and that's exactly where our focus lies.
I’ve always said I’ve focused on the 12 players on our side, but we’re fully aware of what’s facing us on the other side: the best players in the world.”
Happy about the performance his players have accomplished on a difficult golf course, Bjorn continued: "I'd never rush that.
Tomorrow's a different caliber.
Tomorrow, the individual services are in the foreground and this is a completely different thing.
It’s great to work with a partner when things are going well, but when you’re outher alone, you’re tested to the limits of your skills as a golfer.
You have to make this clear to the players: get the most out of yourself tomorrow.
Now you’re leaving your partner behind and he’ll have to get the most out of himself.”
Unlike Bjorn, his counterpart Jim Furyk will make sure that his players score better individually than in the team, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have won three out of four points.
Furyk himself stood on both sides of those major U-turns on the last day of the tournament. He was on the winning side in Brookline before standing on the side of the losers after Europe's "Miracle of Medinah."
“I remember every damn word,” he replied when asked how Ben Crenshaw, the captain in 1999, motivated his players before the last day.
“We’ve got 12 important games tomorrow, but you’d love to take the quick start you’ve seen in Brookline and Medinah.
When momentum strikes in one direction, it puts the middle games under a lot of pressure.
We’re setting up our team accordingly and sending our guys out the way we feel, you know, we’re trying to do magic tomorrow.”
Thomas was given the task of pulling out for counter-striking, he has to compete against Rory McIlroy in the top game, with Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter as other Europeans in the top half of the leaderboard.
"I chose the guys in that order because I think it's covering everything," Bjorn said of his selection for the individual encounters.
Germany's new warship delayed again
The German navy's newest frigate should have been put into service as early as 2014 to replace ageing warships from the Cold War. However, according to local media, it won't be extradited until next year at the earliest due to faulty systems and rapidly growing costs.
The start-upship of the “Rheinland-Palatinate”, the lead ship of the “Baden-Württemberg” class, has now been postponed to the first half of 2019, according to the time referred to a military spokesman.
The ship should have entered service as early as 2014, but the ambitious project was plagued by disturbing post-delivery problems.
The four ships of the Baden-Württemberg class, which were already ordered by the Navy in 2007, are intended to replace the old frigates of the Bremen class.
They are said to include a powerful cannon, a number of anti-aircraft missiles and neagogy missiles, as well as camouflage technologies such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic reflection.
Another important feature is longer maintenance periods. It should be possible that the latest frigates do not have to return to their home ports for up to two years.
However, the ongoing delays may cause the state-of-the-art warships, which are supposed to portray Germany as a navigative power, being obsolete at the time of their commissioning, according to Die Zeit.
The frigate F125 tracked by the misfortune hit the headlines last year when the German navy officially refused to take down the ship and the shipyard Blohm & Voss returned it in Hamburg.
This was the first time the navy returned a ship to the shipyard after its delivery.
Little became known about the reasons for the return, but German media called a number of significant “software and hardware failures” that would have rendered unusable for the new ship in a combat situation.
Software problems are particularly serious, as ships of the Baden-Württemberg class are operated by a crew of only 120 sailors, only about half as many as on the older frigates of the Bremen class.
It has also been revealed that the ship is far too heavy, resultinging performance losses and preventing the navy from being able to carry out upgrading in the future.
The "Rheinland-Palatinate", which weighed 7000 tonnes, was apparently twice as heavy as ships with a similar class used by Germany in the Second World War.
In addition to the faulty hardware, the costs of the entire project, including the training costs of the crew, also became a problem.
Apparently, the cost rose to €3.1 billion, compared to the €2.2 billion originally envisaged.
The problems with the latest frigates are particularly significant in light of recent warnings that Germany's navigity is shrinking.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, military commissioner of the German Bundestag, confirmed that the navigable ships were running out.
Bartels said the problem increased over time, as old ships were taken decommissioned but no replacement vessels had been deployed.
He complained that none of Baden-Württemberg-class frigates could be withdrawn from the Navy.
National Trust listens to the secret lives of bats
New research is being carried out in an estate in the Scottish Highlands to show how bats use the landscape when searching for food.
It is hoped that the results will provide further insights into the behaviour of these unique flying mammals and help guide future conservation activities.
The study, conducted by researchers at the National Trust for Scotland, will trace dwarf and mosquito bats as well as the brown long ear and aquatic bats at Inverewe Garden in Wester Ross.
Special recording devices are set up at key positions on the property to record the activities of bats throughout the season.
NHS employees and volunteers will also carry out mobile investigations using portable detectors.
In the case of a noise analysis of all recordings by experts, the frequency of the bat calls is determined and determines which species does what.
Subsequently, a map and report of the habitat are created to create a detailed picture of their behavior at the landscape level.
Rob Dewar, conservation adviser for the NTS, hopes the results will show which areas of habitat are most important to the bats and how these will be used by each species.
These data will help to determine the benefits of measures to manage habitats, such as the creation of meadows, and to show how to preserve forest areas for bats and other related species.
Bat stocks in Scotland and throughout the United Kingdom have declined significantly in the last century.
They are threatened by construction and development work that affects sleeping places and leads to the loss of habitat.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a risk, as well as fly catchers and the chemical treatment of building materials or attacks by domestic cats.
Bats aren't really blind.
However, due to their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to catching prey.
They use a sophisticated echolocation to detect insects and obstacles in their trajectory.
The NTS, which is responsible for caring over 270 historic buildings, 38 significant gardens and 76,000 acres of land across Scotland, takes the issue of bats very seriously.
He has 10 trained experts who carry out regular examinations, inspections of sleeping places and sometimes rescue efforts.
The organisation has even set up Scotland's first and only bat reserve at the Threave estate in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight of Scotland's 10 bat species.
Estate manager David Thompson explains that the property is the ideal habitat for them.
“Here in Threave we have a great area for bats,” he explains.
“We have the old buildings, many old trees and an excellent habitat.
But there’s a lot we don’t know yet about bats, that’s why we’re doing this work here and on other goods that will help us learn more about what bats need to feel comfortable.”
He stresses the importance of excluding the presence of bats before maintenance work in the goods, as it is possible that the unwilling destruction of a single nesting site could kill up to 400 females and juveniles and wipe out the entire population on site.
Bats are under protection and it is illegal to kill, harass them or to destroy them.
Elisabeth Ferrell, Scottish official of the Bat Conservation Trust, encouraged the population to help.
She explains: "We still have a lot to learn about bats and with many of our species, we don't know what it's about their populations."
Ronaldo denies rape allegations while his lawyers seek to sue German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo described rape accusations against him as “fake news” and said that by using his name “famous”.
His lawyers are planning to sue the news magazine Der Spiegel, which published the allegations.
The striker of the national team of Portugal and Juventus Turin has been accused of raping an American named Kathryn Mayorga in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
Supposedly, he subsequently paid her $375,000 to buy her silence about the incident.
In an Instagram live video to his 142 million-followers, 33-year-old Ronaldo cited the reports as “fake news just hours after their release.”
“No, no, no, no, no.
What they've reported today: fake news," the five-time Ballon d'Or winner said to the camera.
“They want to profile themselves by using my name.
It's normal.
You want to become famous with my name, but that's part of the job.
I'm a happy man and everything's good," the player added with a smile.
Ronaldo's lawyers are preparing to sue the Spiegel on the basis of the accusations they described as "inadmissible reporting on suspicions in the field of privacy," according to Reuters.
Lawyer Christian Schertz said the player demanded damages for "moral damages at an amount equal to the seriousness of the violation, which is believed to be one of the most serious violations of personality rights in recent years."
The alleged incident is said to have occurred in 2009 in a Palms Hotel and Casino suite in Las Vegas.
After getting to know each other at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga allegedly went into the player's room, where he allegedly raped them anally, according to documents submitted to Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga alleges that Ronaldo got down on her knees after the alleged incident and told her he was “99 percent a good guy” who had succumbed to the “one percent”.
In the documents it is alleged that Ronaldo confirms that the two were having sex, but say it was consensual.
Mayorga also maintains that she went to the police and had taken pictures of her injuries in a hospital, but later agreed to an out-of-court settlement as she was “fear of retaliation” and worried about being “publicly humiliated”.
The 34-year-old said she is now trying to repeal the settlement as she is still traumatised due to the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was on the verge of moving from Manchester United to Real Madrid at the time of the alleged incident, switching to Italian giant Juve this summer in a €100 million deal.
Brexit: The UK would 'forever regret' the loss of automakers
The UK would "regret it forever" if it loses its status as a world-leading manufacturer of cars after Brexit, according to Economy Minister Greg Clark.
He went on to say that it was "worrying" that Toyota UK told the BBC that it would temporarily suspend production at the Burnaston plant near Derby should Britain leave the EU.
“We need a deal,” Clark said.
The Japanese automaker stated that the impact of delays at the border in a no-deal brexit could lead to the loss of jobs.
The Burnaston plant, where Toyota's Auris and Avensis product lines are produced, manufactured nearly 150,000 vehicles last year, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the European Union.
“I am convinced that in the event of an uncontrolled Brexit at the end of March, we will see production stops at our plant,” said Marvin Cooke, Toyota’s managing director at Burnaston.
Other British automakers, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rovers, have also raised fears of a tough EU exit without an agreement and its impact on cross-border trade.
BMW, for example, says it will close its mini-plant in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
The main concerns are related to risks within the supply chain in a hard Brexit, according to the automakers.
Toyota's manufacturing facility is powered on just-in-time, which means the parts are delivered every 37 minutes by suppliers from both the UK and the EU to manufacture the cars to order.
Should the UK leave the EU without an agreement on March 29, there could be interruptions at the borders that could lead to delays and bottlenecks in parts, according to the industry.
It is impossible for Toyota to maintain an inventory at the Derbyshire plant that will last longer than one day, the company explained, which is why production must be interrupted.
Clark explained that Theresa May's Chequers plan over the future relationship with the EU is "exactly designed to avoid such checks at the border."
"We need a deal. We want the best deal that, as I said, allows us not only to enjoy the current success, but also to seize this opportunity," he explained on BBC Radio 4's Today.
“The findings not only from Toyota but also from other manufacturers show that we must be able to continue the previously very successful supply chains.”
Toyota couldn't specify how long production would have to be disrupted, but warned in the long term that additional costs would reduce the plant's competitiveness and eventually lead to job losses.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has been employed at Burnaston for 24 years and is the highest functionary of the Unite union, explained that his members are increasingly worried: “In my experience, these jobs will never come back when they are gone.
A government spokesman said: "We have put forward a precise and credible plan for our future relationship with the EU."
Trump's planned meeting with Rosenstein could be postponed again, according to White House data
Donald Trump's important meeting with Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could be postponed "by another week," while the dispute over Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh continues, it said Sunday from the White House.
Rosenstein has overseeing the work of special counsel Robert Mueller, which is investigating Russia's electoral meddles, links between Trump's advisers and Russia, as well as a possible obstruction of justice by the president.
The question of whether Trump would fire the vice-prisoner, thereby jeopardizing Mueller's independence, has been the subject of rumors in Washington for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein was addressing the possible possibility of secret recording of his conversations with Trump, as well as impeaching the president's impeancement using the 25th Amendment.
Rosenstein denied the report.
However, he went to the White House last Monday, while new reports of his alleged resignation were revealed.
Instead, a meeting with Trump, who was at the U.N. General Assembly in New York at that time, was announced for next Thursday,
Trump declared that he would prefer Rosenstein not dismissive, but the meeting was then postponeded to avoid colliding with the hearing before the U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee, in which both Kavanaugh and Dr Christine Blasey Ford, who testified to him for sexual abuse.
On Friday, Trump ordered a week-long FBI investigation into the allegations against Kavanaugh, further delayinging the Senate's full vote.
Trump's press secretary Sarah Sanders appeared on Fox News on Sunday.
Speaking to a question about the meeting with Rosenstein, she replied: “A date hasn’t yet been set for it. It could be this week, but I can also imagine that it’ll be postponed for another week, given all the things going on with the Supreme Court.
That will become apparent, but I’m always happy to keep up to date.”
Some reporters arguably disagree with this assertion: Sanders hasn't helded a White House press conference since September 10.
Presenter Chris Wallace asked her why.
Sanders explained that the low frequency of press conferences did not lay on an aversity to the “effectives” of the TV reporters, but admitted: “I wouldn’t deny that they’re engaging in effect-hazing.”
She then asserted that direct contact between Trump and the press would increase.
The president does more question-and-answer rounds than any president before him," she declared, without providing accurate evidence: "We've looked at these numbers."
Press conferences will still take place, according to Sanders, however, "if the press has the opportunity to directly ask the President of the United States, but far better than talking to me.
We try to do that frequently and you've seen we've done this often in recent weeks. That's going to take the place of the press conference if you can talk to the President of the United States."
Trump regularly accepts questions when he leaves the White House or attends open meetings or press conferences with dignitaries visiting.
Press conferences with him alone alone are rare.
In New York, perhaps, the president showed why this is the case when he made an out-of-the-art and sometimes bizarre appearance in front of assembled reporters.
Health secretary writes to Scottish NHS EU workers because of Brexit concerns
The health secretary is addressing people from other EU countries working in Scotland's National Health Service (NHS) to express the country's gratitude and express a desire to remain in the country even after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, a member of the Scottish Parliament, sented a letter less than six months before the UK's departure from the EU.
The Scottish Government has already agreed to bear the cost of applications for residence permits for EU citizens working in Scotland's decentralised public service.
In her letter, Freeman wrote: "Over the summer, exit negotiations between the UK and the EU continued and decisions are expected this autumn.
However, the UK government has also stepped up preparations for a possible no-deal scenario.
I'm aware of what disturbing times these must be for all of you.
That is why I wanted to express once again how much I value the contribution of each and every employee, regardless of nationality.
Colleagues from all over the EU and beyond bring valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of the health service and thus benefit the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is fully your home and we would be very happy if you would stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie underwent emergency surgery after a head injury
The linebaker of the Tennessee State Tigers Christion Abercrombie underwent emergency surgery, according to Tennessee State's Mike Organ, after a head injury he contracteded in Saturday's 31-27 defeat to Vanderbilt Commodores.
Rod Reed, the head coach at Tennessee State, explained to reporters that the injury occurred just before the half-time break.
"He came to the sidelines and just collapsed there," Reed said.
Trainers and medical staff supplied Abercrombie with oxygen on the sidelines before being placed on a stretcher and transported away for further examinations.
A Tennessee State spokesperson told WSMV's WSMV, Tennessee that Abercrombie has left the operation at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that "no details of the nature or extent of the injury have yet been known and Tennessee State is trying to figure out when the injury occurred.
Abercrombie, a so-called redshirt, is set in his first season at Tennessee State after moving from Illinois.
Before leaving the game on Saturday, he recorded a total of 5 tackles, bringing his total of 18 tackles in the season.
Foreign investors will have to pay higher stample duty for property buying
Investors from abroad will have to impose a higher stample duty for the purchase of property in the UK in the future. The additional revenues are expected to benefit Conservative Party homeless
This move is intended to curb the success of Jeremy Corbyn's campaign around young voters
Increasing the stamp duty will affect everyone who does not pay taxes in the United Kingdom
Financiary expects additional revenue of up to £120 million a year to help homeless people
Theresa May will announce today that foreign investors will have to pay a higher stampling fee if you buy property in the United Kingdom, benefitinging the additional revenue homeless.
This move represents an attempt to curb Jeremy Corbyn's success among young voters, which promises to provide more affordable housing and target top earners.
The increase in stamp duty will be levied on individuals and corporations that do not pay taxes in the United Kingdom. The additional revenues are to be donated to the government campaign against homelessness.
The surcharge, which will be levied in addition to the current stamp duty and including the increase introduced two years ago on second homes and residential properties leviated, could be up to three percent.
As a result of the move, the Ministry of Finance expects additional revenue of up to £120 million per year.
An estimated 13 per cent of newly built properties in London are bought by investors who aren't based in the United Kingdom, driving up prices and making it harder for first-time buyers to gain a foothold in the real estate business.
Many prosperous areas of the country, especially in the capital, have become “ghost towns” due to the high number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the United Kingdom.
The new strategy follows just weeks after Boris Johnson advocated a reduction in stample duty to allow more young people their first home.
He accused construction companies of holding up property prices by buying up land without using it, urging Theresa May to give up quotas on affordable housing to end Britain's "living space disgrace."
Opposition leader Corbyn announced a sensational series of proposals for housing reforms, ranging from rent fixing to banning evictions for no valid reason.
He also wants to give city councillors more powers to build new apartments.
“Last year I said that I would dedicate my time as Prime Minister to the restoration of the British dream, according to which life should become better from generation to generation.
And that means repairing our broken housing market.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live and work here and want to build a life.
However, it may not be right that it is just as easy for people who do not live in the UK, as well as for companies based abroad, to buy property as it is for hard-working British citizens.
The dream of homeownership has become too distant for too many people and the unworthiness of homelessness remains too big a problem.”
Jack Ross: 'My biggest goal is to manage Scotland'
Jack Ross, Sunderland's boss, says his 'biggest goal' is to eventually become the Scottish team's manager.
The 42-year-old Scot enjoys the challenge of breathing new life into the club from the north-east of England, which is just in third place in League One and missing three points to the top.
He moved to the Stadium of Light this summer after leading St Mirren back to the first Scottish league last season.
“I wanted to play as a player for my country.
I made it to the bench and that's it," Ross told BBC Scotland's Sportsound show.
“But as a child I have often seen Scotland play in Hampden with my father and this is something that always pulls me back.
However, this possibility would only come if I were successful in club management.”
Ros's predecessors in Sunderland's role as manager include Dock Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Alloa Athletic's former boss claimed not to be afraid to follow in the footsteps of such established names at such a club, having previously rejected offers from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
The success will at the moment be measured by whether I can return the club to the Premier League.
Given the structure and opportunities in this club, he no doubt belongs in the Premier League," he explained.
“It’s not an easy task to get him there, but I’d probably only consider my time here a success if I brought the club back there.”
Ross has a three-year management career, after a phase as deputy boss at Dumbarton and a 15-monthe-long career as a coach at Hearts.
Then he helped Alloa recover from relegation to the third tier and helped St Mirren from the edge of relegation to winning the championship the following season.
Ross says he now feels more comfortable than ever before in his playing career at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
“That was probably a real crossroad,” he recalls when he took on Alloa’s responsibility.
“I really believe that management was the right thing for me, even more than playing.
It sounds funny, because I didn't queue up badly, was able to live on it well and experienced some very pleasant high phases.
However, playing can be tough.
There are a lot of things you have to go through every week.
I'm still doing that in terms of stress and pressure in my job, but management feels right.
I've always wanted to manage and now I'm doing it. I've never felt so comfortable in my skin in my entire life as an adult."
You can listen to the whole interview on Sunday 30 September between 12:00 and 13:00 BST at Sportsound on Radio Scotland.
The perfect time for a glass of beer is Saturday at 5:30 p.m., according to a study
Summer's heat wave has given an upswing to Britain's crisp-ridden pub revenues, while restaurant chains came under greater pressure.
Pub and bar companies recorded sales growth of 2.7 percent in July. However, revenues at restaurants fell 4.8 percent, according to statistics.
Peter Martin of the management consultancy CGA, which compiled the figures, explained: “Continuing sunshine and England’s longer-than-expected participation in the World Cup meant that July followed a similar pattern to the previous June, in which pubs recorded sales increases of 2.8 percent. However, restaurant hit it even harder.
The 1.8 per cent decline the restaurant industry recorded in June continued to increase in July.
Pubs and bars focusing on beverages showed by far the best performance, with an area-adjusted sales development that rose higher than it dropped at restaurants.
Pubs with an emphasis on fooding also suffered in the sun, but not as drastically as the restaurant operators.
It seems like people were just going out for a drink.
In managed pubs and bars, beverage sales increased 6.6 per cent per month, while food sales declined by three percent.”
Paul Newman, RSM’s analysts, said: “These results are continuing the trend we have seen since the end of April.
The weather and the influence of important social or sporting events remain the biggest factors for sales in the out-of-home market.
It's not surprising that restaurant groups continue to struggle with problems, however revenue losses of 4.8 per cent compared to the previous year will be particularly painful.
The long, hot summer could not have come at a worse time for operators of companies focusing on food, and it remains to be seen if August’s more temperate temperatures will provide much-needed respite.”
Revenue growth overall for pubs and restaurants, including new openings, was 2.7 per cent in July, reflecting the decline in brand rollouts.
The industry revenue monitor Coffer Peach Tracker for the UK's pub, bar and restaurant sector collects and analyzes performance data from 47 active companies with combined sales of over £9 billion and is the recognised industry benchmark.
One in five children owns secrectared accounts on social media that it hides from its parents
A study shows that one in five children, some just 11 years old, owns secret accounts on social media that it keeps secret from parents and teachers
A survey of 20,000 pupils at secondary schools has noticed an increase in so-called “fake Insta” sites
The news fuels fears that sexual content is being posted
Twenty percent of students reported owning a “main account” to show it to their parents
One in five children, some just 11 years old, creates accounts on social media that it can keep secret from adults.
A survey of 20,000 pupils at secondary schools showed a rapid increase in so-called “fake Insta” accounts, a reference to photo-sharing website Instagram.
The news fuels fears that sexual content will be shared.
Twenty percent of pupils said they have a clean “main account” that they can show their parents, but also have private accounts.
One mother who came across the secret side of her 13-year-old daughter found a teenen who urged others to "rape me."
The study, conducted by Digital Awareness UK and the Headmasters’ and Headmistresses’ Conference (HMC) of independent schools, found that 40 percent of 11 to 18-year-olds had two profiles, with half admitting to owning private accounts.
Mike Buchanan, chairman of HMC, said: “It’s disturbing that so many teenagers are tempted to create online spaces where parents and teachers can’t find them.”
Eilidh Doyle becomes 'voice of the athletes' in Scottish sports council
Eilidh Doyle was elected as a non-executive member of the Scottish Athletics Federation's Scottish Athletics at its annual general meeting.
Doyle is Scotland's most award-winning athlete and chairman Ian Beattie described the choice as a great opportunity for the sports officials to benefit from her far-reaching international experience over the past decade.
“Eilidh is respected in the athletics community of Scotland, Britain and the world and we are sure that athletics in Scotland would benefit greatly from their election to the council,” Beattie said.
Doyle said: "I look forward to acting as a voice of the athletes and hope that I can make a real contribution and guide the sport in Scotland."
The American, who won the distance over 200 and 400 meters at the Atlanta Olympics in 1996, owns a total of four Olympic gold medals and now frequently appears as a BBC pundit, could no longer run after a temporary circulatory disorder.
He tweeted: "A month agove I suffered a stroke.
I couldn't walk anymore.
Doctors say that no one can say whether or how far I'm going to recover.
It was hard work, but I have fully recovered, learned to walk again and do mobility exercises today!
Thank you very much for the encouraging news!”
An advertisement for breast pumps comparing mothers to cows is causing a stir on the net
A manufacturer of breast pumps caused a stir on the net with an advertising clip in which breastfeeding mothers are compared to the milking of cows.
In order to launch the “world’s first low-noise portable breast pump”, the manufacturer of consumer technology Elvie published a not quite serious music video to emphasize the freedom that the pump gives breastfeeding mothers.
Four real mothers dance in a hay-filled barn with cows in the background to a song that contains lines of lyrics such as “yes, I’m milking myself, but you don’t see a cock on me” or “if you didn’t notice it, they’re udders but my boobs.”
“Pump it out, pump it out, pump it out, I feed the babies, pump it out it, pump it out, I’m milking my ladies.”
However, the advertisement posted on the company's Facebook page has sparked controversy on the net.
With 77,00 views and hundreds of comments, the video encountered mixed reactions from viewers, many of which say that it trivializes the “terrifications” of the dairy industry.
“It was a very bad decision to promote this product with cows.
Just like us, they must become pregnant and give birth to a calf to produce milk, with the difference that their cubs are taken away from them within a few days of giving birth," a comment said.
Elvie's mash pump discreetly fits into a nursing bra (Elvie/Mother)
Another comment read: “Comprehensibly traumatic for mother and baby.
But hey, why don’t we use them to apply a breast pump for mothers who are allowed to keep their babies?”
One other person added: “Such an unrealistic advertisement.”
Others defended the advertisement and a woman admitted that she found the song “hilarious.”
“I think it’s a brilliant idea.
I'd have bought one if I was still breastfeeding.
While pumpinging, I felt exactly like a cow.
The clip's a little crazy, but I'm not trying to overinterpret it.
It's an ingenious product," one person wrote.
Another person commented: “This is a witty advertisement aimed at mothers who use breast pumps (often in the workplace or on toilets) and feel like “cows”.
It’s not an advertisement for or against the dairy industry.”
At the end of the video, the group reveals to women that everyone danced with the discrete pumps in their bras.
The concept behind the campaign is based on the realisation that many women who use breast pumps claim to feel like dairy cows.
Elvie's pump, however, is completely noiseless, has no wires or hoses and fits discreetly into a nursing bra, giving women the freedom to move, keep their babies, or even go out during the pumping process.
Ana Balarin, partner and ECD at Mother, said: “Elvie’s pump is such a revolutionary product that it deserves a bold and provocative launch.
By comparing breastfeeding mothers with dairy cows, we wanted to focus on breast pumps and all associated challenges and at the same time introduce the incredible sense of freedom that the new pump makes possible in an entertaining and comprehensible way.
This isn't the first time Elvie's pump has hit the headlines.
During London Fashion Week, a mother-of-two appeared on the catwalk for designer Marta Jakubowski while using the product.
Hundreds of migrant children were quietly taken to a tent city on the Texas border
The number of interned migrant children is higher than ever before, although the number of monthly border crossings has remained relatively the same, among other things as tightened rhetoric and action by the Trump administration have made it more difficult to accommodate children with carers.
Traditionally, most caregivers themselves have been illegal immigrants and are now afraid to become a target for expulsion themselves if they come forward to take in a child.
The risk increased in June, when federal authorities announced that potential carers and other adult members of their household would have to give their fingerprints and that data would be shared with immigration authorities.
Last week, Matthew Albence, a senior civil servant of the Immigration and Customs Enforcement, declared to Congress that the agency arrested dozens of people who had applied to care for unaccompanied minors.
The authority later confirmed that 70 percent of those arrested did not have any previous entriements to the criminal record.
“Nearly 80 percent of people who are either caregivers themselves or a member of a caregiver’s household reside illegally in the country and a large proportion of them are delinquent foreigners.
That's why we will continue to persecute these individuals," Albence explained.
In order to be able to process children's cases more quickly, officials introduced new rules, which, according to employees of the shelters, requires some children to appear in trial within a month instead of 60 days of detention, which was previously the standard.
Many will appear via video and not in person to stand up in front of an immigration judge for their legal status.
In case of negative decisions, the person is quickly deported.
The longer these children remain in custody, the more likely it is, according to the accommodation staff, as well as reports learned from the system in the last month that they are becoming anxious and depressed, which can lead to violent outbreaks or outbreak attempts.
Defenders say these concerns are heightened at a larger facility like Tornillo, as there signs that a child is having problems are more easily overlooked due to size.
They also explained that it could intensify trauma that many of the children are already suffering from being taken to a tent city without emotional preparation or sufficient time to say goodbye to friends.
Syria urges American, French and Turkish 'occupiation forces' to withdraw immediately
In a speech to the U.N. General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem also called on Syrian refugees to return home, although the country's civil war is now in its eighth year.
Moualem, who is also deputy prime minister, declared that the foreign armed forces were illegally on Syrian soil with the pretext of fighting terrorism and were “dealing accordingly.”
“They must withdraw immediately and unconditionally,” he explained to the assembly.
Moualem insisted that the “war on terror is almost over” in Syria, where more than 360,000 people have died since 2011 and millions more people have been displaced from their homes.
He said Damascus "will continue to fight this holy battle until all Syrian areas are purged" both from terrorist groups as well as "any illegal foreign presence."
The United States has stationed about 2000 soldiers in Syria who are mainly training and advising both Kurdish forces as well as Syrian Arabs hired against President Bashar al-Assad.
France has more than 1000 ground troops in the war-torn country.
With regard to the refugees, Moualem stated that the conditions for their return are given and he accused “some Western countries” of “spreading irrational fears” that prevent refugees from going home.
"We have called on the international community and humanitarian organizations to make their homecoming possible," he said.
“They are politicizing an actually purely humanitarian matter.”
The United States and the European Union warned no build-up aid would be granted for Syria as long as there was no political agreement between Assad and the opposition to end the war.
U.N. diplomats stated that the recent agreement between Russia and Turkey on a buffer zone in the last major rebel stronghold of Idlib constituted the possibility of advancing political talks.
The deal between Russia and Turkey averted a wide-ranging offensive by Russian-backed Syrian armed forces to the province where three million people live.
Moualem stressed, however, that the agreement had “clear deadlines” and expressed his hope that military action against jihadists, including fighters from the Al-Qaeda-allied Nusra Front, which “will be wiped out.”
U.N. representative Staffan de Mistura hopes to soon convene initial meetings of a new committee consisting of government and opposition members to draft a post-war constitution for Syria and paving the way to elections.
Moualem cited the Syrian government's conditions for participating in the committee and stated that its work should be confined to "revising the articles of the current constitution" and warned of interference.
Why Trump will win his re-election
With this logic, Trump would win his re-election in 2020, shouldn't, as many liberal viewers presumably hope, an impeachment or a scandal prematurely end his presidency.
This would undoubtedly be “the most dramatic finale of a presidency of all time.”
So far, there's no indications that viewers are tired of it.
Since 2014, ratings at the best broadcast time at CNN have more than doubled to 1.05 million and nearly tripled at MSNBC to 1.6 million.
According to Nielsen, Fox News reaches an average of 2.4 million viewers at the best airtime, compared to 1.7 million four years ago. MSNBC's "The Rachel Maddow Show" led the ratings with up to 3.5 million viewers on evenings with important news.
"It's like a fire that magically attracts people as it's something we don't understand," said Neal Bear, showrunner of the drama series Designated Survivor aired on ABC about a cabinet member who becomes president after an attack destroys the Capitol.
Nell Scovell, a veteran comedy writer and author of Just the Funny Parts: And a Few Hard Truths About Sneaking Into the Hollywood Boys’ Club, has a different theory.
She remembers a cab ride in Boston before the 2016 election.
The driver told her that he would choose Trump.
She asked him about the reason.
“He said, ‘because he makes me laugh’,” Scovell told me.
The chaos has entertainment value.
Of course, unlike all that can be found on television, the stories from Washington can determine the future of the fundamental decision on abortion Roe v. Wade, the possible merging of families, and the health of the global economy.
Switching off is a luxury that only the most privileged spectators can afford.
And yet it goes beyond being an informed citizen when you become aware of having already watched six hours of a panel of experts discussing Bob Woodward's use of sources from the so-called "Deep Background" for his book "Fear," Paul Manafort's $15,000 ostrich-leather bomber jacket (according to Washington Post a "clothing piece of self-exaggeration") or the effects of Stormy Daniel's tearful description.
For my part I'll never be able to see Super Mario with the same eyes.
“Part of what he’s doing makes it feel like a reality show he puts in front of you every night,” says Brent Montgomery, chief executive of Wheelhouse Entertainment and creator of the series “The Three of the Pawn House,” about Trump’s rotating cast and daily plot turns (breaking a dispute with the football league NFL from the fence, Kim Jong-un praise).
You can't afford to miss an episode, otherwise you won't come along.
When I reached diligence this week, it was sunny 27 degrees in front of his house on the northern coast of the Hawaiian island Kauai, but he crawled inside and watched MSNBC while recording CNN at the same time.
He just couldn't detach himself from the screen, not when judge candidate Brett Kavanaugh was on the verge of testifying before the Senate Judiciary Committee and the future of the Supreme Court was at stake.
"I remember when we were doing all these crazy broadcasts and people were saying 'This is the beginning of the end of Western civilisation,'" Fleiss told me.
“I thought it was some kind of joke, but it turned out they were right.”
