Donations flood the tightest races for seats in Congress in midterm elections
It's unsurprising that Pennsylvania's 17th electoral district is flooded by donations, thanks to the new-drawn boundaries of electoral districts that caused two incumbents in the race for the same seat.
The recently-drawn suburban district in Pittsburg lets Democrat Conor Lamb, who won his seat in another district in the wake of a special election the previous spring.
Lamb is competing against another incumbent, Republican Keith Rothfus, who currently represents the old 12th district of Pennsylvania, which overlaps sharply with the new 17th district.
The cards were redrawn after the Pennsylvania State Supreme Court ruled in January that the old counties violated the Constitution and were manipulated in favor of Republicans.
The race in the new 17th district sparked an exchange of campaign funding between the parties' major financial weapons, the Democratic Campaign Congressional Committee (DCCC) and the National Republican Campaign Committee (NRCC).
Lamb became known in Pennsylvania after a narrow victory in a much-hypeded special electoral election in March around the state's 18th electoral district.
