The U.S. provised El Salvador's punishment for its support for China but then retreated
Diplomats noted that the Dominican Republic and Panama had already recognized Beijing without encountering great resistance from Washington.
Trump graduated from a friendly meeting with President Panama's Juan Carlos Valero in June 2017 and owned a hotel in Panama until partners threw out the Trump Organization's management team.
U.S. State Department officials have decided to withdraw the heads of all U.S. missions abroad from El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama due to “recent decisions to stop recognizing Taiwan,” said Heather Nauert, spokeswoman for the ministry in a statement earlier this month.
However, it was only in the case of El Salvador that punitive measures, which received an estimated $140 million in $140 million in 2017, including for drug controls, development and economic aid.
The proposed punitive measures, including cuts in financial assistance and targeted visa restrictions, would have been painful for the Central American country and its high unemployment and homicide rates.
As internal meetings progressed, North and Central American officials postponed a high-level conference on security and economic prosperity that was supposed to follow a similar gathering the previous year and represented a step forward in efforts to stop migrants from moving towards the U.S.
In mid-September, however, senior government officials made it clear that they wanted the conference to take place and thus effectively ended the consideration of punitive measures for El Salvador.
Vice-President Mike Pence is now scheduled as a spokesperson at the mid-October conference to set a sign of the importance that the government is giving to the assembly.
The three American diplomatic representatives have quietly returned to El Salvador, Panama and to the Dominican Republic, with no new harsh notifications or punitive measures from Washington.
A John Bolton government spokesperson declined to comment on the details of the debate described by the three American officials, including two diplomats who, on condition of anonymity, agreed to commenting on the internal meetings.
Their comments were reaffirmed by an external analyst close to the government, who also spoke on the condition of anonymity.
Learning from history
The next big surprise could be special counsel Robert Mueller's report on Trump's possible impediment to justice, for which there is now sufficient evidence in public.
Mueller is also reportedly investigating whether Trump's campaign team conspired with Russia in its attack on the American elections.
Should the Congressional majority change, Trump would be confronted with an organ forcing him to be accountabout at the very time when he's heading past to stand in front of voters again; and possibly before a jury.
That’s a lot of ‘ifs’ and I don’t want to say that Donald Trump’s case, or that of his European counterparts, is inevitable.
On both sides of the Atlantic, we must make decisions that will affect how long the fight will last.
In 1938, German officers were ready to carry out a coup d’état against Hitler if the West had merely resisted him and supported the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
We have failed, and missed a way to avoid the years of barbarism that followed.
The course of history revolves around such turning points and the unstoppable march of democracy is either accelerated or delayed.
Americans are facing several such turning points right now.
What do we do when Trump dismisses Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, the man who decides the future of Mueller's investigation?
Rosenstein fell into Teufel's kitchen since this newspaper reported last year that he proposed to secretly record the president and speculated about being unsuitable for office.
Rosenstein denies the New York Times' portrayal.
“How will we respond if Brett Kavanaugh’s re-arranged FBI investigation isn’t complete or fair, or he’s being confirmed despite credible accusations of sexual abuse and misstatement in office?
And most importantly, are we going to vote in the midterm elections for a Congress forcing Trump to account?
If we fail in these trials, a long winter is imminent.
But I don't think we're going to fail because of what I've learned in Prague.
My mother was a Czechoslovak Jew who was deported to Auschwitz by the same regime that had once occupied my embassy home.
She survived, immigrated to America and, 60 years later, sent me untighing Sabbath candles on the table with the swastika.
How can I not be optimistic with this heritage?”
Norman Eisen, Senior Fellow at the Brookings Institution, is head of the Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington and author of The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House.
Graham Dorrans of the Rangers optimistic before the match against Vienna
The Rangers receive Rapid Vienna on Thursday in the knowledge that a win against the Austrians after the impressive draw against Villareal in Spain earlier this month will put them in a good position for qualifying Group G of the Europa League.
Due to a knee injury, Graham Dorrans could not make an appearance until the 2-2 tie against Villareal, but he believes the Rangers can use this result as a stepping stone for greaters.
“This was an important point for us, because Villareal is very good,” said the 31-year-old.
We've gone into the game with the conviction that we can make something out of it and we've gone home with a point.
Maybe we could still have won at the end, but overall a draw was probably a fair result.
They presumably dominated in the first half, but we struggled in the second half and were then the better team.
Thursday will be another big European evening.
Hopefully we get three points, but it will be a difficult game, as they have achieved a good result in the last game, but I am sure that we can do this with the audience behind us and achieve a positive result.
Last year was definitely difficult, with everything that happened with my injuries and the changes at the club itself, but now there's some sort of feel-good factor.
The team is good and the guys are in a really good mood; the training is good.
Hopefully we can look forward now, leave last season behind and be successful.”
Fear of retirement savings leads to sleepless nights in women.
Although participants in the survey knew exactly how to take care of them, few spoke to their family members about it.
Approximately half of participants in Nationwide's study reported talking to their spouses about the cost of long-term care.
Only 10 percent said they were talking to their children about it.
“People want a family member to take care of them, but don’t take the necessary steps to have the conversation,” said Holly Snyder, vice-president of Nationwide’s life insurance division.
You should start with this.
Talk to your spouse and the children: you can't prepare your family to care for you if you don't express your desires on time.
Work with your adviser and your family to discuss where and how you want to be cared for, as these decisions can be a significant factor in determining costs.
Get your financial adviser on board: your advisor can also help you find a way to finance these expenses.
Your long-term care financial decisions may include traditional long-term care insurance, a hybrid capital-forming life insurance to help cover those costs or self-insurance with your own assets - as long as they own the money.
Work out your legal documents: Avert litigation at the last minute.
Set up a precautionary power of attorney so that you hire a trusted person to monitor your medical care and to ensure that health professionals comply with your desires if you are unable to express it yourself.
Also consider a power of attorney for your finances.
You select a familiar person who makes financial decisions for you and makes sure that your bills are paided should you not be able to.
Don’t forget the details: Imagine that your elderly parent has a medical emergency and is on their way to the hospital.
Would you be able to answer questions about taking medications or allergies?
Note these details in a written plan so you’re ready.
“It’s not just the financial issues that play a role. Who are the doctors?” Martin asked.
“What is drug treatment?”
Who'll take care of the dog?
You have already completed this plan.”
Man in Ilfracombe multiple times shelled with air rifle
A man has been shelled with an air rifle on several occasions as he went home after working with friends.
The victim, a man in his forties, was in the Oxford Grove area in Ilfracombe, Devon, when he was shot in the chest, belly and hand.
Officials described the shots, which occurred at about 02:30 British time, as an "arbitrary act."
The victim did not see the attacker.
His injuries weren't life-threatening and police are looking for witnesses.
Earthquakes and Tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people were killed by a strong earthquake and a tsunami that encountered the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, according to authorities. The number of dead is still likely to rise.
Because of the failed communication systems, aid workers were unable to obtain information from Donggala's government district, an area north of Palu located closer to the epicenter of the magnitude 7.5 earthquake.
In Palu, over 16,600 people were evacuated after the disaster.
Here are some important facts about Palu and Donggala on Sulawesi Island:
Palu is the capital of central Sulawesi province and is located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of the island of Sulawesi, with an estimated population of 379,800 in 2017.
The city was just celebrating its 40th anniversary as the earthquake and tsunami occurred.
Donggala is a administrative district that extends to the northwest of the island of Sulawesi over a coastal section of over 300 km (180 miles) in length.
An estimated 299,200 people lived in the administrative district, an administrative unit below the provincial district.
Fisheries and agriculture are the main sources of income of the Central Sulawesis economy, in particular the coastal region of Donggala.
Nickel mining is also important in the province, but it focuses mainly on Morowali, on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been victims of earthquakes and tsunamis several times over the past 100 years, according to Indonesia's disaster management agency.
In 1938, over 200 people were killed by a tsunami that destroyed hundreds of homes in Donggala.
West Donggala was hit by a tsunami in which nine people died in 1996.
Indonesia sits on the seismic Pacific ring of fire, and is regularly plagued by earthquakes.
Here are some of the biggest earthquakes and tsunamis of recent years:
2004: A powerful earthquake on the west coast of Indonesia's Aceh province in northern Sumatra triggered a tsunami on December 26 that hit 14 countries and killed 226,000 people in the coastal areas of the Indian Ocean, nearly half of them in Aceh.
20005: A series of major earthquakes shook the west coast of Sumatra between late March and early April.
Hundreds of people died on Nia's islands off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude earthquake shook southern Java, Indonesia's most populous island, triggering a tsunami that hit the south coast, claiming nearly 700 deaths.
2009: An earthquake with a magnitude of 7.6 hit the town of Padang, the capital of western Sumatra province.
Over 1,100 people were killed.
201010: An earthquake with a magnitude of 7.5 rocked one of the Mentawai islands off Sumatra and triggered a tsunami of up to 10 metres in height that destroyed dozens of villages and claimed about 300 lives.
2016: A slight earthquake shooned Pidie Jaya in Aceh and caused destruction and panic when people were reminiscent of the destruction of the deadly earthquake and tsunami of 2004.
No tsunami was triggered this time, but over 100 people died from collapsing buildings.
2018: Severe earthquakes hit the Indonesian island Lombok, popular with tourists, causing over 500 deaths, especially on the northern side of the island.
The quake destroyed thousands of buildings and thousands of tourists were temporarily stranded on the island.
Sarah Palin's son arrested on allegations of domestic violence
Track Palin, the eldest son of Alaska's former governor and former nominee for the vice president's office, has been arrested due to allegations of assault.
29-year-old Palin from Wasilla, Alaska, has been arrested on suspicion of domestic violence, obstruction of reporting domestic violence and resisting state violence, according to a report released by Alaska police on Saturday.
According to the police report, he took the phone from an acquaintance when she wanted to inform the police.
Palin is in pre-trial detention at the Mat-Su Pretrial Facility, according to KTUU, and a $500 bail has been levieled.
He appeared in trial on Saturday, where, when asked about his plea, he replied "not guilty, quite sure," according to the network.
Palin is accused of committing three Class A misdemeanories, which can be punished with up to a year's imprisonment and a fine of up to $250,000.
He is also accused of a Class B administrative offense, which can be punished with a day in prison and a fine of $ 2,000.
It's not the first time that criminal charges have been filed against Palin.
In December 2017, he was accused of assaulting his father Todd Palin.
His mother, Sarah Palin, called the police to report the alleged attack.
The case is currently being negotiated in front of the Veteran’s Court of Alaska.
In January 2016 he was accused of domestic violence, obstruction of reporting a criminal offence and possessing weapons under narcotive influence in connection with the incident.
His girlfriend accused him of punching her in the face.
Sarah Palin was criticized by veteran organizations in 2016 after she attributed her son's behavior to post-traumatic stress disorder as a result of his time in Iraq.
Tsunami after earthquake in Indonesia: Hundreds dead
At least 384 people died after an earthquake that rocked the Indonesian island of Sulawesi on Friday.
The quake with a magnitude of 7.5 triggered a tsunami that destroyed thousands of houses.
Electricity and communications networks are disrupted and it is expected that the number of deaths will increase in the coming days.
The earthquake occurred just off the coast of central Sulawesis, located northeast of Indonesia's capital Jakarta.
Videos circulate on the social networks from when the wave hit the country.
Hundreds of people had gathered for a beach festival in the town of Palu when the tsunami hit the coast.
Federal Prosecutors Call for Rare Death Penalty for Suspect in New York Terrorist Attack
Federal Prosecutor's Office in New York is demanding the death penalty for Sayfullo Saipov, the defendant in the New York City terrorist attack that cost eight lives. This is a rare sentence that hasn't been imposed for a federal crime in the state since 1953.
The 30-year-old Saipov is accused of having used a rented pick-up of the hardware store chain Home Depot for a terrorist attack, in the course of which he raced onto a bicycle path along the West Side Highway in Lower Manhattan in the course in the process, running over pedestrians and cyclists.
To justify the death penalty, prosecutors must prove that Saipov “targetedly” killed the eight furnace and “consciously” inflicted serious bodily harm, according to the memorandum of understanding for the demeand of the death penalty filed in the southern district of New York.
Both charges could be punished with death, according to the court document.
Weeks after the attack, a federal oath court of Saipov presented an indictment on 22 points, which includes eight charges of murder against the backdrop of organized crime, which are usually used by federal prosecutors in cases of organized crime, as well as an indictment of the exercise of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack required “fundamental planning and intent,” according to the accusers, who described the manner in which Saipov carried out the act as “abhorrent, cruel and depraved.”
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov harmed the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco.
Five victims were Argentinian tourists.
For more than a decade, the southern district of New York no longer pursued a case with the aim of demanding the death penalty.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was pleaded guilty to the murder of two drug suppliers and eventually sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last time the death penalty was carried out in New York during a federal offense in 1953 in the case of Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple guilty two years earlier of conspiracy to commit espionage for the Soviet Union.
Both Rosenbergs were executed with an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, who was born in Uzbekistan, testified to a lack of remorse in the days and months after the attack, according to court documents.
He told investigators that he felt good about what he did, according to the police.
Saipov explained to authorities that he was inspired to the act after watching videos of ISIS on his phone, according to the indictment.
According to the police, he also demanded to hang the flag of ISIS in his hospital room.
He pleaded not guilty on all 22 charges.
David Patton, one of the federal public defenders representing Saipov, declared that they were "of course disappointed" because of the prosecution's decision.
“We believe that the decision to demand the death penalty, instead of accepting a confession of guilt in exchange for a life imprisonment without the possibility of dismissal, will only prolong the trauma that everyone involved suffers,” Patton said.
Saipov's defense squad had previously asked the indictment not to claim the death penalty.
