Elite League: Dundee Stars 5-3 Belfast Giants
Patrick Dwyer scored two goals for the Giants against Dundee
The Dundee Stars made up for Friday's defeat in the Elite League against the Belfast Giants through a 5:3 victory in the return match at Dundee on Saturday.
The Giants went into the lead early with two goals, after hits from Patrick Dwyer and Francis Beauvillier.
Mike Sullivan and Jordan Cownie equalised for the home team before Dwyer restored the Giants' lead.
Francois Bouchard equalised for Dundee before Lukas Lundvald Nielsen secured victory with two goals.
This was for the men of Adam Keefe, who unexpectedly beat Dundee on Friday night 2-1 in Belfast, the third defeat in the Elite League in the season.
This was the fourth clash of the season between the two teams, with the Giants winning the three previous games.
Dwyer's opening goal came in the fourth minute after 3:35 minutes after a submission from Kendall McFaull. Four minutes later David Rutherford delivered the template when Beauvillier eased the lead.
In this exciting opening period, Sullivan brought the home team back into the game after 13:10, before Matt Marquardt helped Cownie to his equalising goal after 15:16 minutes.
Dwyer secured the Giants' lead before the half-time break when he scored his second goal of the evening just before the end of the first half.
The home team regrouped and Bouchard equalised again with a power-play hit in the 28th minute.
Cownie and Charles Corcoran combined to help Nielsen put Dundee in the lead for the first time in the game late in the second half. Nielsen then secured his team's victory with a fifth goal after barely halfway through the second half.
The Giants, who have now lost four of their last five games, are playing home at Milton Keyne's stadium next Friday.
Air traffic controller died after making sure that hundreds of people on a plane escaped the earthquake
An air traffic controller in Indonesia is hailed as a hero after he died to safely launch a plane with hundreds of people on board.
Over 800 people have died and many are missing after the island of Sulawesi was rocked on Friday by a devastating earthquake triggering a tsunami.
Strong aftershocks are still felt in the region and many people are trapped under debris in the city of Palu.
Although his colleagues fled to save your lives, 21-year-old Anthonius Gunawan Agung refused to leave his post in the strongly fluctuating control tower at Mutiara Sis Al Jufri airport in Palu.
He stayed to make sure Batik Air's flight 6321, which was on the runway at the time, was able to take off safely.
He then jumped out of the tower when he thought he was collapsing.
Agung later died in the hospital.
Yohannes Sirait, spokesman for Indonesia's aviation safety service, explained that this decision may have saved hundreds of lives, according to Australia's ABC News.
“We provided a helicopter from Balikpapan in Kalimantan to bring it a larger hospital in another city.
Unfortunately, he died this morning before the helicopter could arrive in Palu.
We are deeply affected by this news,” he added.
At the same time, the authorities fear that the number of dead could rise to several thousand. The country's civil protection agency stated that access to the cities of Donggala, Sigi and Boutong is limited.
“The number is expected to increase, as many bodies are still under the debris, while others cannot be reached,” Sutopo Purwo Nugroho said.
Waves with a height of up to 6 metres have ravaged Palu, which will hold a mass funeral on Sunday.
Military and commercial aircraft bring relief supplies and supplies.
Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, told Sky News: “Every minute comes a new ambulance with the dead.
Clean drinking water is scarce.
Supermarkets are plundered everywhere.”
Jan Gelfand, head of Indonesia's International Red Cross, told CNN: "The Indonesian Red Cross rushes to help survivors, but we don't know what they'll find there.
It’s a tragedy now, but it could get much worse.”
Indonesia's President Joko Widodo arrived in Palu on Sunday and declared in an address to the country's military: "I ask you all to work day and night to do all the tasks associated with evacuation.
“Since you’ve been ready?”
Indonesia has already been rocked this year by earthquakes in Lombok, in which over 550 people died.
