Number of North Korean defectors 'sinks' under Kim
The number of North Korean defectors to South Korea has dropped since Kim Jong-un came to power seven years ago, according to a South Korean politician.
Park Byeong-seug, referring to South Korea's reunification ministry data, stated that 1127 defectors were registered last year, compared to 2706 in 2011.
Park cited stricter border controls between North Korea and China, as well as higher prices of human smugglers as the main factors.
Pyongyang did not comment publicly.
The vast majority of defectors from the north are eventually offered South Korean citizenship.
Seoul states that over 30,000 North Koreans have crossed the border illegally since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most are fleeinging via China, which has the longest border with North Korea, which is easier to cross than the heavily protected demilitarized zone (DMZ) between the two Korean states.
China considers defectors as illegal immigrants rather than refugees and often leads them back by force.
Relations between the North and the South, which are technically still in a state of war, have improved significantly in recent months.
