“That wasn’t a well-thought-out decision,” she told me 30 years later with a laughter.
There were already seven other candidates in the race and the last thing they needed was another candidate.
Someone called the situation “Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.”
Since the election campaign had already progressed, she was behind in raising funds and she vowed not to run if she didn't get $2 million together.
It was a hopeless struggle.
She found that some of her supporters who donated $1000 to men supported their campaign only with $250.
“Do they think I’m getting a discount?” she wondered.
In the speech, in which she announced she would not launch an official campaign, she was so overwhelmed by her emotions, gratitude for her supporters, frustration over the system, which made the raising of funds and direct addressing of voters so difficult instead of the delegates, as well as the anger about sexism, so overwhelmed that she was close to tears.
“It could have been assumed that I suffered a nervous breakdown,” Schroeder recalls of the press reactions.
“It could have been believed that handkerchief manufacturer Kleenex was my sponsor.
I still recognised that I wondered what they'd write on my tombstone.
“She was crying?”
Why Beijing could benefit from U.S.-China trade war
The opening salvos of the U.S.-China trade war were deafening. Although the battle is far from over, Beijing could benefit from a trench between countries in the long term, experts say.
Donald Trump, the American president, fired the first warning shot earlier this year when he punitive tariffed key Chinese export goods, including solar cells, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation occurred this week with new punitive tariffs on $200 billion worth of goods, effectively affecting half of all goods imported into the U.S.
Beijing struck back in the same way each time and recently imposed $60 billion worth of punitive tariffs of five to ten percent on American goods.
China vowed to answer any U.S. measure and it's unlikely that the world's second-largest economic power will stumble soon.
Promoting Washington to step in would mean giving in. However, a public buckling towards the U.S. would be far too shameful for China's President Xi Jinping.
Nevertheless, experts think that if Beijing plays its cards well, China could benefit in the long term from the effects of the trade war by reducing the interdependence of the two economies.
“The fact that a quick political decision, either in Washington or Beijing, could create conditions for economic turbulence in both countries is actually much more dangerous than many observers have recognized before,” says Abigail Grace, a research associate focused on Asia at the Center for New American Security think tank.
Syria is 'ready' for the return of the refugees, according to the foreign minister
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and asks for help for the reconstruction of the country devastated by the seven-year-long war.
In an address to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem stated that circumstances in the country are improving.
"Today the situation on the ground is more stable and safer thanks to the progress made in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government is rebuilding the areas destroyed by terrorists in order to return to normality.
All conditions are now given for the voluntary return of refugees to the country, which they had to leave because of terrorism and unilateral economic measures aimed at their everyday lives and livelihoods.
The UN estimates that over 5.5 million Syrians have left the country since the start of the war in 2011.
Another six million people who still live in the country need humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian government welcomes assistance in rebuilding the devastated country.
He stressed, however, that it would not accept conditional relief from countries that had supported the uprising.
Europe achieved victory in the Ryder Cup in Paris
Team Europe won the 2018 Ryder Cup and defeated Team USA with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at Le Golf National near Paris.
The U.S. has now lost six consecutive times on European soil and has not won a Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe regained the crown after Danish captain Thomas Bjorn's team reached the 14.5 points it needed to defeat the U.S.
American star Phil Mickelson, who had problems over much of the tournament, sunk his tee shot at the 16th lane (par 3) in the water and lost to Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari was able to shine in all his rounds and became one of 4 players who scored a 5-0-0 since the tournament began in its current format in 1979.
American Jordan Spieth was beaten 5:4 by the player with the lowest ranking in Team Europe, Thorbjorn Olesen of Denmark.
The player with the world's best ranking, Dustin Johnson, lost 2 to 1 against Englishman Ian Poulter, who may have played his last Ryder Cup.
Spaniard Sergio Garcia, veteran from eight Ryder Cups, became the best European Ryder Cup player of all time with 25.5 career points.
“Normally I don’t cry, but today I couldn’t hold back.
It's been a difficult year.
I'm so grateful that Thomas chose me and believed in me.
I'm so infinitely happy to win back the trophy.
It's about the team and I'm happy to have made a contribution," said emotional Garcia after the European victory.
He passes the torch further to his compatriot John Rahm, who won against American golf legend Tiger Woods 2:1 in the individual encounters on Sunday.
“I’m so incredibly proud to have beaten Tiger Woods that I’ve been watching on TV since my childhood,” said 23-year-old Rahm.
Woods lost all of his four matches in France and now has a Ryder Cup record of 13-21-3.
A curious statistic for one of the greatest players of all time who has won 14 major titles and is only surpassed by Jack Nicklaus.
Team U.S. had trouble finding the fairways all weekend, with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who made high-profile performances throughout the tournament.
American captain Jim Furyk said after a disappointing performance by his team: "I'm proud of the boys, they fought.
There was a time this morning when we pressured Europe.
We didn't make anything out of it.
Hats off for Thomas.
He's a great captain.
All of his 12 players put in a good performance.
We're going to regroup, I'm going to talk to the PGA America and our Ryder Cup committee and we'll look forward.
I love these 12 guys and I'm proud to be their captain.
You have to pull your hat.
We were defeated.”
Red Tide Update: Concentrations in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota Decrease
The Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission's latest report shows that there is a general decline in red tide concentrations in parts of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, more patchy flowering conditions are being recorded in the areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier, indicating decreasing concentrations.
A red algae bloom stretches over approximately 130 miles of coastline from the north of Pinella to the south of Lee district.
Spots can be found about 10 miles off the coast of the Hillsborough district, but in fewer places compared to previous weeks.
A red tide has also been observed in Pasco district.
In the last week, medium concentrations were reported in or off the coast of Pinellas district, low to high concentrations off the coast of Manatee district, background to high concentrations in or off the coast of the Charlotte district, background to high concentrations in or off the coast of Lee district and low concentrations in Collier district.
Respiratory complaints continue to be reported from the districts of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
No new respiratory problems became known in northwest Florida over the course of last week.
